"""Original applied lessons for the complete TIH Liberian Law programme.
All scenarios are fictional. Videos illustrate concepts; Liberian primary sources govern.
The module sequence follows the existing foundation stage without moving its saved steps.
"""
EXTRA_LESSONS = []
def add(module, title, source, reading, definition, teaching, example, terms, assignment, questions, video, exam_advice, activities):
    EXTRA_LESSONS.append(dict(module=module, title=title, source=source, reading=reading,
        definition=definition, teaching=teaching, example=example, distinction=terms,
        assignment=assignment, questions=questions, video=video, examAdvice=exam_advice,
        activities=activities, level='Applied legal studies'))

add(22, 'Liberian Courts, Jurisdiction and Access to Justice', 'courts',
 'Read the Judiciary Law with constitutional Articles 65 and 66. Locate the jurisdiction provisions for the relevant trial court rather than importing foreign monetary limits.',
 'Original jurisdiction is authority to hear a matter at first instance; appellate jurisdiction is authority to review a lower decision. Subject-matter, territorial and personal jurisdiction answer different questions. Access to justice also involves practical ability to understand and use a lawful process.',
 'Start a forum analysis with the kind of dispute, parties and remedy. A circuit court, magistrate court and specialised court are not interchangeable merely because they are nearby. The Constitution places judicial power within the judiciary and provides the Supreme Court’s appellate role; legislation determines the competence of subordinate courts.\n\nSeparate an official’s job title from a court’s adjudicative power. A ministry may investigate or administer a statute without being the final judicial forum. A mediator facilitates settlement and does not acquire judicial authority through the parties’ convenience. Map possible routes, then verify the enabling text, procedural entry requirements and available review. Legal merit alone does not cure an incompetent forum. An access plan should identify language, records, transport and lawful assistance without promising a particular result.',
 'Fictional case: a Gbarnga trader brings a disputed equipment contract to a local office because it is closer than a court. The officer suggests asking a chief to decide it. A weak answer treats distance as jurisdiction. A strong answer identifies the contracting parties, requested relief and place of performance; checks the competent court under Liberian law; and distinguishes voluntary mediation from a binding judicial decision. If the transaction has a customary-land component, analyse that component separately instead of assuming one institution can decide everything.',
 [('Original jurisdiction','Power to determine a matter at first instance'),('Appellate jurisdiction','Power to review a lower decision'),('Access to justice','Practical and lawful ability to seek a remedy')],
 'Prepare a two-page forum map for the trader. For each possible institution record its legal source, jurisdiction question, entry documents and review route. Add an accessibility plan.', [
 'A nearby office offers to decide a contract claim. What establishes its adjudicative competence?|An applicable legal grant of jurisdiction|Its convenient location alone|The consent of one claimant alone|Its role in collecting local taxes|Convenience and administrative responsibility do not themselves create judicial power.',
 'Which issue differs from whether a claimant has a strong contract case?|Whether the selected court has jurisdiction|Whether delivery was agreed|Whether payment was made|Whether performance was defective|Forum competence is independent of the merits of the underlying claim.',
 'What is a sound access-to-justice plan for a learner’s fictional case?|Identify lawful assistance, required records and practical barriers|Promise that court access guarantees victory|Replace all procedural requirements with a complaint letter|Ask an unrelated administrator to issue judgment|An access plan removes practical barriers while respecting competent procedures.',
 'A dispute involves both a contract and a claimed customary-land right. What is the best forum analysis?|Check the legal competence for each component and their relationship|Assume the nearest official decides every component|Treat mediation as a Supreme Court appeal|Assume a customary issue eliminates constitutional safeguards|Mixed issues require an authority-based analysis of the relevant institutions.'
 ], 'IGyx5UEwgtA',
 'Organise the answer by subject matter, parties, territory and remedy. Give a sourced forum conclusion and identify any fact that prevents a final jurisdiction determination.',
 ['Allocate the trader’s issues among three hypothetical institutions and challenge each allocation with its required source.', 'Rewrite an inaccessible filing checklist into plain language without changing its legal requirements.'])

add(22, 'Judicial Precedent, Ratio and Distinguishing Cases', 'courts',
 'Locate a published Liberian Supreme Court opinion through the Judiciary. Read the material facts, issue, disposition and reasons together; record a verifiable pinpoint citation.',
 'The ratio decidendi is reasoning necessary to the decision on its material issues. Obiter observations are comments not necessary to that result. Distinguishing explains why a material difference prevents a prior rule from resolving the present issue in the same way.',
 'A case is more than its final sentence. First identify what the court had to decide, the facts it treated as material, and the legal proposition connecting those facts to the outcome. Then assess the court’s place in the relevant legal hierarchy and later treatment of the proposition. A sentence quoted out of context may describe a party’s argument rather than the court’s holding.\n\nDistinguishing requires a legally significant difference, not simply a different county, year or party name. State the old rule, identify the changed fact, and explain why the rule’s reasoning depends on that fact. An analogy does the reverse: explain why the important similarities justify applying the reasoning. A foreign judgment may help develop an argument, but its persuasive value must not be presented as a binding Liberian holding. Never manufacture a local citation to make a classroom example appear authoritative.',
 'Fictional exercise: Published Case A, obtained by the learner, concerns a signed guarantee. The classroom variation contains no guarantee and the supplier dealt only with the company. The weak response repeats the earlier result because both files involve debt. The strong response asks whether personal authorisation was material to the actual decision, accurately states that decision’s reasoning, and tests whether the absence of a guarantee changes the liability analysis. Case A is a placeholder for the real opinion the learner selects, not an invented Liberian case.',
 [('Ratio decidendi','Reasoning necessary to decide a material issue'),('Obiter dictum','Observation not necessary to the result'),('Distinguishing','Explaining a material difference limiting an earlier analogy')],
 'Write an 800-word precedent analysis using one verified Supreme Court opinion. Include one analogy, one proposed distinction and a paragraph identifying what the opinion does not decide.', [
 'A quoted passage merely records counsel’s submission. How should it be used?|Identify it as an argument, not automatically the court’s holding|Present it as binding because it is printed in a judgment|Treat it as a newly enacted statute|Assume it overrules every earlier case|The role of a passage within the reasons determines what it actually establishes.',
 'Which difference most plausibly supports distinguishing a guarantee case?|The new transaction lacks the personal guarantee material to the earlier reasoning|The new debtor lives in another county|The judgment uses an older font|The classroom parties have different names|A distinction must change a fact relevant to the legal reasoning.',
 'What should accompany an analogy to a foreign judgment?|A clear account of its jurisdiction and persuasive status|An invented Liberian case number|A claim that all common-law courts are interchangeable|An assertion that English language alone makes it binding|Comparative reasoning must disclose the authority’s actual status.',
 'A judgment’s broad remark goes beyond the issue necessary for its result. What must the learner examine?|Whether the remark is obiter rather than the controlling ratio|Whether every sentence has identical precedential force|Whether the remark automatically becomes legislation|Whether a summary replaces the full opinion|Necessary reasoning and incidental observations have different analytical roles.'
 ], 'GJ6qB4gVbns',
 'State the rule in context, connect it to the court’s material facts, and explain the proposed analogy or distinction. Cite the actual opinion rather than a textbook’s unsupported summary.',
 ['Mark a public judgment in three colours: facts, party arguments and court reasoning.', 'Exchange a case brief with a partner and test whether its claimed ratio is supported by the cited passage.'])

add(23, 'Predictive Legal Memoranda and Opinion Writing', 'irac',
 'Use the legal problem-solving guide and a verified Liberian authority relevant to a fictional issue. Keep the memo’s research log beside its draft.',
 'A predictive memorandum evaluates likely legal outcomes for its reader, including weaknesses and uncertainty. An advocacy submission argues a party’s position. Both require accurate authority, but their purpose and tone differ.',
 'Frame a neutral question that includes the legally material facts. “Was the dismissal unlawful?” is less useful than a question identifying the employment relationship, alleged reason and disputed procedure. A memorandum normally separates question, short answer, relevant facts, governing rules, application and conclusion. Headings should expose the reasoning rather than disguise gaps in it.\n\nApply each element or procedural requirement to evidence supporting and opposing the proposed conclusion. A counterargument belongs in the discussion, not in a footnote pretending it does not exist. Distinguish an unknown fact from an uncertain rule: the former calls for factual investigation, the latter for additional authority or candid qualification. A reasoned opinion also explains the route from issue to disposition. Neither document should promise an outcome unsupported by the record. Update the short answer after the analysis is complete.',
 'Fictional case: a Paynesville worker was dismissed after a disputed stock shortage, but the employer’s written notice and the employee’s response have not been obtained. A weak memo guarantees reinstatement after quoting a foreign video. A strong memo identifies the applicable Liberian employment framework, separates proof of shortage from the decision process, examines the worker’s contrary account and concludes conditionally. Its research section identifies which documents and current provisions would change that assessment.',
 [('Predictive memorandum','Balanced analysis of likely outcomes and uncertainty'),('Advocacy submission','Reasoned presentation supporting a party’s position'),('Material fact','A fact capable of affecting a legal element or result')],
 'Write a 1,000-word predictive memo for the dismissal scenario with a neutral question, brief answer, issue headings, counterargument and a research-and-evidence gaps table.', [
 'What distinguishes a predictive memo from a one-sided advocacy draft?|It evaluates relevant weaknesses and competing outcomes candidly|It omits all conclusions|It avoids primary authority|It must support whichever party pays the writer|Predictive advice requires balanced assessment, even when an adverse argument is strong.',
 'The notice of dismissal is missing. How should the memo treat the asserted reason?|Identify it as an evidential gap requiring the actual record|Assume the worker’s summary is a final court finding|Invent a notice matching the desired result|Replace the notice with a foreign policy template|The analysis should disclose the difference between available evidence and an assumption.',
 'When is the best time to finalise the memo’s short answer?|After the substantive analysis has tested the evidence and rules|Before reading any relevant authority|Once a desired outcome is chosen|After deleting all counterarguments|The short answer should accurately reflect the completed reasoning.',
 'A memo confuses an unknown fact with an unsettled legal issue. What is the useful correction?|Separate the factual inquiry from the additional legal research needed|Treat both gaps as established facts|Remove the issue from the memo|Guarantee that more words will resolve both|Different kinds of uncertainty require different next steps.'
 ], 'Io5ttZ0ih9w',
 'Use a question tied to material facts, a rule for each issue and a developed application. Conclude with the strongest supported answer and the exact limits of confidence.',
 ['Convert a partisan complaint into a neutral memo question and fact statement.', 'Peer-review a memo paragraph by identifying its rule, application, counterargument and unsupported assumption.'])

add(23, 'Client Interviewing, Case Planning and File Management', 'ethics',
 'Read the Liberian professional ethics code and court rules relevant to record handling. This lesson uses a simulated interview, not unsupervised legal representation.',
 'A legal interview gathers a reliable account, clarifies the person’s objective and identifies records, deadlines and conflicts. Case planning turns that account into an issues-and-actions file. Confidentiality is an ethical duty; legal privilege is a separate rule requiring its own conditions.',
 'Begin a simulation by explaining the learner’s role and limitations. Check who seeks assistance, who else is involved and whether conflicting interests require referral before collecting unnecessary sensitive details. Use open questions for the initial account, focused questions for dates and documents, and a summary to test understanding. Avoid suggesting the answer through a leading question.\n\nSeparate what the speaker saw, what another person reported and what the speaker inferred. Build a chronology with the source for each entry. A file should include an issues list, document index, research tasks and a calendar based on verified local requirements; a foreign video cannot establish a Liberian filing deadline. Keep originals intact, note copies and versions, restrict access and record lawful consent for any recording. A plan is useful only if it assigns a next action to the information gap it is meant to resolve.',
 'Fictional case: a Buchanan tenant says “the owner cheated me last month” and produces a cropped receipt. The weak interviewer accepts the accusation and drafts a claim immediately. The strong interviewer asks when payment was made, who received it, what was agreed, whether there is a complete receipt and what outcome is sought. The file labels the alleged conduct as an allegation, preserves the cropped image and seeks the original. The learner explains that a TIH course does not authorise private legal practice.',
 [('Open question','Question inviting an account in the speaker’s own words'),('Chronology','Dated sequence with identified sources'),('Confidentiality','Duty to protect information, distinct from evidential privilege')],
 'Conduct a 15-minute fictional interview. Produce a chronology, a document index, a conflict-check note and a one-page action plan with five targeted factual questions.', [
 'Which initial question best supports an unprompted account?|“Please describe what happened in your own words.”|“The landlord lied, did he not?”|“You want me to guarantee compensation, correct?”|“Why did the other party commit fraud?”|An open question gathers the speaker’s account without presupposing wrongdoing.',
 'A speaker repeats what a neighbour told them. How should the file record it?|As a reported account with the neighbour identified as its source|As the speaker’s direct observation|As a judicial finding|As verified documentary proof|Source attribution helps distinguish knowledge from inference or hearsay.',
 'What is the sound response to a cropped receipt?|Preserve it and seek the complete original and surrounding records|Edit the crop to hide its limitations|Assume it proves every contract term|Replace it with a newly written receipt|Integrity and completeness must be investigated without altering the available evidence.',
 'A simulated interview reveals opposing parties may both seek help. What comes first?|Assess the conflict and role limitations before proceeding|Collect each party’s secrets and combine them|Promise both parties representation|Assume shared geography eliminates conflicts|Conflict management protects the parties and the integrity of assistance.'
 ], '6d6HU0_dV34',
 'Turn the facts into an issues-and-evidence plan. Explain why each missing document or question matters, and keep professional-authorisation limits distinct from interviewing skills.',
 ['Practise open, focused and confirming questions using an entirely fictional tenant file.', 'Create a document index that distinguishes originals, copies, edited files and missing records.'])

add(24, 'Judicial Review and Constitutional Remedies', 'constitution',
 'Read constitutional Articles 2, 20, 65 and 66 alongside the Judiciary Law and applicable court rules. Identify the appropriate local procedure and remedy for a challenged public act.',
 'Judicial review examines a public act against the governing legal limits. A merits appeal may ask a reviewing body to reconsider a result under a particular statutory scheme. A constitutional remedy responds to an established constitutional problem through a competent process.',
 'Identify the challenged act, its maker and its claimed source of power. Test statutory competence, procedural duties and applicable rights separately. A decision can involve a lawful objective yet be made by the wrong official or through a defective procedure. Equally, disagreement with a policy does not itself establish a constitutional violation.\n\nA remedy must match the defect and the court’s powers. Setting aside an act, requiring a lawful hearing, restraining threatened action and compensating a loss are different requests with different foundations. Consider who is entitled to bring the challenge, what record supports it and whether a specialised review route applies. Do not copy foreign writ names or time limits into a Liberian answer without local authority. Explain what the proposed order would do and what it would leave to the responsible institution.',
 'Fictional case: a licensing office cancels a Monrovia clinic’s permission after an inspection but refuses to identify the legal power or disclose the relied-on findings. A weak answer declares that all inspections are unconstitutional. A strong answer investigates the enabling law, the cancellation procedure and the affected interests. It distinguishes the authority to inspect from the authority to cancel and proposes a procedurally appropriate challenge while reserving any merits conclusion until the inspection record is available.',
 [('Legality review','Examination of power and legal limits'),('Merits review','Reconsideration of substance under an available review scheme'),('Remedy','Legally authorised response to an established wrong')],
 'Write a 900-word review memorandum and a draft requested-relief section for the clinic, citing verified local sources and listing unresolved procedural questions.', [
 'Which question is central to a legality challenge?|Whether the decision-maker acted within its lawful power|Whether the learner prefers another policy|Whether all inspections are unpopular|Whether the office has a modern building|Judicial review requires a legal defect, not mere policy disagreement.',
 'Why separate inspection authority from cancellation authority?|Different acts may require different legal powers and procedures|One official always has every related power|Inspection automatically proves every alleged breach|Cancellation is never reviewable|An enabling statute must support the particular act being challenged.',
 'A requested order would require a new lawful hearing. What must be distinguished?|Restoring a process from guaranteeing a favourable substantive result|A hearing from a source of legislation|A hearing from automatic compensation|A hearing from abolishing the regulator|Procedural relief need not settle the eventual merits.',
 'A student copies a foreign judicial-review time limit. What should the answer do instead?|Verify the applicable Liberian procedure and temporal requirements|Assume common language makes the limit identical|Use whichever foreign deadline is longer|Ignore all filing requirements|Local authority determines the available route and its requirements.'
 ], 'c2q0yVdGbhg',
 'Divide the answer into power, procedure, rights and remedy. Connect the requested order to the identified defect and acknowledge any unresolved route or standing question.',
 ['Sort the clinic’s allegations into jurisdiction, procedure, evidence and policy disagreements.', 'Draft two alternative remedies and explain the legal and practical difference between them.'])

add(24, 'Constitutional Amendment and Democratic Participation', 'constitution',
 'Read Articles 91–93 on amendment with relevant provisions on legislative authority, expression and association. Verify current electoral legislation and official notices before discussing an actual election.',
 'An ordinary Act operates within the Constitution; an amendment changes the constitutional framework through its prescribed process. Democratic participation includes lawful expression, association, representation and scrutiny of public decisions.',
 'Separate the power to propose change from the steps needed to make it legally effective. A speech, petition, legislative proposal and completed constitutional amendment have different status. Identify the constitutional actor responsible for each stage, the formal requirements and the point at which a proposed change would take effect. Popular support cannot by itself replace a prescribed legal procedure.\n\nParticipation also requires distinguishing political disagreement from a supported legal complaint. Explain which right, institution and process are involved. A restriction on an assembly or publication must be analysed against the relevant constitutional text and lawful procedures, not merely against the preferences of its critics. In an election problem, keep voter eligibility, candidate eligibility, administration, results and a legal challenge separate. Check the current governing instruments instead of treating campaign statements as law.',
 'Fictional case: an organisation in Kakata gathers signatures supporting a change to a constitutional rule, and a speaker announces that the Constitution has already changed. The weak answer accepts the announcement as an amendment. The strong answer identifies the signatures as one form of participation, reads the amendment provisions, determines which formal steps remain, and explains why the existing constitutional text continues to govern until lawful change occurs. It does not invent a referendum result or a numeric threshold.',
 [('Ordinary legislation','An Act made within the constitutional framework'),('Constitutional amendment','Change adopted through prescribed constitutional procedures'),('Participation','Lawful involvement in public deliberation and institutional processes')],
 'Prepare a sourced amendment-process diagram and a 700-word public explainer correcting the speaker’s announcement without discouraging lawful participation.', [
 'Why does strong public support not itself complete an amendment?|The prescribed constitutional procedure must still be satisfied|Public participation is always unlawful|An ordinary official can waive every formal step|A petition automatically repeals the Constitution|Legitimacy of support and legal effectiveness of amendment are distinct questions.',
 'Which document is the starting point for an amendment-process analysis?|The constitutional amendment provisions|A campaign poster alone|A foreign referendum manual alone|A summary of social-media reactions|The Constitution defines the required institutional route.',
 'An election problem combines registration and a results challenge. What improves the analysis?|Separate the legal issues and governing processes for each stage|Treat every disagreement as one identical claim|Assume candidate eligibility proves the result|Replace official records with speeches|Different election stages can involve different duties, records and remedies.',
 'How should a public explainer describe a proposal not yet lawfully adopted?|As a proposal with identified remaining constitutional steps|As an immediately effective constitutional amendment|As proof the existing text is irrelevant|As a binding Supreme Court judgment|Accurate status language prevents a proposal from being mistaken for current law.'
 ], 'AQNi6ieu3D8',
 'Distinguish proposal, lawful process and effective change. Cite the Liberian text for each required step; use comparative video examples only to illuminate the distinction.',
 ['Place a petition, bill, referendum proposal and effective amendment on a status timeline.', 'Role-play a civic-information session distinguishing a political argument from a legal claim.'])

add(25, 'Interlocutory Applications, Injunctions and Case Management', 'civil',
 'Locate provisional-relief and motion provisions in the Civil Procedure Law and relevant court rules. Verify the applicable test, notice requirements and authority for the precise application.',
 'An interlocutory application seeks an order during a proceeding rather than its final determination. An injunction can restrain or require conduct under the governing law. Case management organises the steps necessary for a fair and efficient decision.',
 'Begin with the purpose of the proposed order: preserve property, protect an evidential record, prevent a threatened wrong or organise disclosure. Identify the court’s power and the elements of the local test. Evidence of urgency is different from an assertion that the applicant will eventually win. Address the effect on the respondent and any safeguards the law requires.\n\nAn interim order should be sufficiently specific to comply with and supervise. “Do justice” is not an operative command. Distinguish a temporary protective measure from a final remedy after proof. Explain notice, supporting evidence and the hearing process from current Liberian sources; an emergency does not erase the need to investigate lawful safeguards. A timetable should connect issues to pleadings, records, witnesses and hearing dates rather than simply postpone unresolved questions.',
 'Fictional case: two parties dispute machinery in a rented workshop, and one plans to move it before the dispute is heard. A weak application asks the court to award permanent ownership immediately because removal sounds unfair. A strong application specifies what property and conduct are at risk, supplies the available evidence, identifies the lawful interim test and requests a defined protective order while leaving disputed title for proper determination.',
 [('Interim relief','Protection during proceedings under the applicable test'),('Final relief','Remedy following determination of the substantive claim'),('Case management','Organisation of procedural steps and evidence')],
 'Draft a fictional interim-relief outline with the requested order, legal test, evidence table, respondent’s counterargument and proposed procedural safeguards.', [
 'What distinguishes urgency from proof of the underlying claim?|Urgency concerns immediate risk; merits require their own supported determination|Urgency automatically proves ownership|Urgency removes all jurisdiction questions|Urgency creates a final judgment|Interim protection and final entitlement are separate analytical questions.',
 'Which proposed injunction is easier to assess and supervise?|An order defining the identified machinery and restrained removal conduct|A request that everyone act fairly|An order transferring all disputed assets without analysis|An order copied from an unrelated foreign form|Specific relief exposes its scope, legal basis and practical consequences.',
 'Why address the respondent’s position in an interim application?|The applicable test and safeguards require assessment of competing interests|The applicant must abandon every claim|The respondent decides jurisdiction|An interim order is always a voluntary contract|A balanced application considers both risk and lawful procedural protection.',
 'What should guide a case-management timetable?|The disputed issues, necessary records and governing procedural requirements|The applicant’s preferred outcome alone|The length of the pleading alone|The assumption that evidence can be organised after judgment|Procedural sequencing should help establish the facts needed for determination.'
 ], 'mHG1bL8KMI0',
 'Identify the interim test, apply each requirement to evidence, and draft a precise order. Explain why the requested measure does not prematurely decide a contested merits issue.',
 ['Convert three vague requests into specific proposed interim orders.', 'Build a case timetable linking each step to a disputed issue and its necessary evidence.'])

add(25, 'Judgment Enforcement, Execution and Appeal Records', 'civil',
 'Read the Civil Procedure Law and court rules governing judgments, enforcement and appeals. Do not assume that filing an appeal automatically produces a stay.',
 'A judgment determines legal rights or obligations; execution is the lawful process for enforcing it. An appeal record documents the matters required for review. A stay suspends specified action under its legal terms.',
 'Read the operative part of the judgment before planning enforcement. Identify the obligated party, precise relief, conditions and relevant procedural status. A monetary judgment, possession order and direction to perform an act call for different enforcement analysis. Information about assets does not itself authorise their seizure. Third-party ownership and applicable exemptions or protections must be assessed.\n\nKeep an appeal’s existence separate from the effect of a valid stay or other order. Locate current requirements for initiating review and preparing the record, and identify the exact alleged errors. Enforcement through a competent court is different from taking property privately. Preserve the judgment, service records, payment history and relevant subsequent orders; missing documents can affect both execution and review. Never import a foreign bailiff’s powers or a filing deadline without Liberian authority.',
 'Fictional case: a supplier wins a payment judgment against a company and asks friends to seize its director’s private motorbike. The company says it has “appealed”, but provides no filing or stay record. A weak answer approves seizure because a debt exists. A strong answer checks the judgment debtor, lawful execution route, ownership of the proposed asset and actual appellate status. It distinguishes a company debt from a director’s personal liability and asks for the relevant orders before reaching a conclusion.',
 [('Execution','Lawful enforcement of a judgment'),('Stay','Legally effective suspension within its stated scope'),('Appeal record','Documents and matters required for review')],
 'Prepare an enforcement-and-review checklist for the supplier, including asset ownership, operative relief, orders, service records and disputed legal grounds.', [
 'A judgment names the company as debtor. What must be checked before targeting a director’s property?|A legal basis for reaching that asset and the director’s liability|The director’s association with the company alone|Whether the motorbike is easy to find|Whether the supplier dislikes the director|The identity of the judgment debtor and asset ownership matter to execution.',
 'What does an assertion that an appeal was filed establish by itself?|A claim requiring verification of the filing and any effective stay|Automatic suspension of every enforcement step|Automatic reversal of the judgment|Personal liability of every shareholder|Filing status and its legal consequences must be proved from the actual procedure and orders.',
 'Why preserve a payment history after judgment?|It helps establish the remaining enforceable obligation|It changes the judgment into legislation|It authorises private seizure|It replaces the appeal record|Execution should address the obligation still outstanding rather than an unsupported original figure.',
 'Which action appropriately distinguishes enforcement from self-help?|Use the authorised process and verify the property subject to it|Take any valuable item linked to the debtor’s family|Treat a copy of the judgment as unlimited seizure power|Ignore later orders because the creditor first won|Enforcement powers and their limits come from the governing law and orders.'
 ], 'kTnOMXEbPGY',
 'Read the operative judgment, identify the debtor and remedy, then analyse execution and review separately. State which filing, ownership or order remains unverified.',
 ['Annotate a fictional judgment to distinguish reasons from operative relief.', 'Audit a mock enforcement file for a missing ownership record, payment and subsequent order.'])

add(26, 'Contract Terms, Interpretation and Exclusion Clauses', 'commercial',
 'Use the Liberian Commercial Code for a transaction within its scope and verify local authority relevant to interpretation and incorporated terms. Do not treat foreign consumer legislation as Liberian law.',
 'An express term is stated by the parties; an implied term arises through an applicable legal rule or recognised basis. Incorporation asks whether a term forms part of the agreement. Interpretation asks what an established term means.',
 'Identify the agreement’s documents and chronology before reading one clause in isolation. A limitation printed after acceptance may raise an incorporation question before any question about its meaning. Distinguish a promise, representation, condition and allocation of risk using the governing legal framework. Read definitions, cross-references and the contract as a whole.\n\nAn exclusion clause does not automatically answer every claim. Establish whether it became part of the contract, what conduct or loss it covers and whether applicable law limits its effect. “The seller is not liable” is different from a defined limitation tied to a particular event and remedy. An ambiguity calls for a reasoned interpretation supported by the transaction and legal rules, not a rewrite creating whichever term a party now wants. Keep interpretive uncertainty separate from a missing agreement document.',
 'Fictional case: a Robertsport buyer agrees an equipment order by email. After payment, an invoice states “all liability excluded”, and the seller relies on it when the equipment fails. A weak answer assumes invoice wording controls because it looks official. A strong answer reconstructs formation, asks when and how the wording was communicated and accepted, examines its scope and any mandatory local rules, and distinguishes incorporation from interpretation and enforceability.',
 [('Incorporation','Whether wording became part of the agreement'),('Interpretation','Meaning of an established contractual term'),('Exclusion clause','Wording limiting responsibility subject to applicable law')],
 'Write an 800-word clause analysis and draft an alternative term that clearly identifies delivery, inspection, responsibility and available remedies without claiming guaranteed enforceability.', [
 'A limitation first appears on an invoice after acceptance. Which issue arises before its meaning?|Whether it was incorporated into the agreement|Whether every invoice changes the law|Whether all seller liability disappears|Whether the buyer’s complaint is criminal|A term must form part of the contract before its scope can determine obligations.',
 'What is the strongest method of interpreting a disputed clause?|Read its wording, definitions, context and applicable legal rules together|Use the clause title alone|Choose whichever meaning benefits the author|Replace missing words with a foreign statute automatically|Interpretation is a reasoned examination of an established agreement.',
 'An exclusion mentions delay but the claim concerns defective goods. What must be analysed?|Whether the wording actually covers that type of breach and loss|Whether any exclusion covers every possible claim|Whether delay and defects are identical|Whether the invoice paper is signed in blue|The clause’s scope must be connected to the specific alleged wrong.',
 'Why distinguish an implied term from an unspoken personal expectation?|An implied obligation requires a recognised legal basis|Every expectation is automatically contractual|Only written obligations can ever exist|A disappointed party may create terms after breach|Contractual obligations must be established through agreement or an applicable implication rule.'
 ], 'mQhg4CjHS08',
 'Answer incorporation, interpretation and legal effect in that order. Apply the actual wording to the alleged breach instead of assuming all exclusions have the same scope.',
 ['Sort a fictional email chain into formation, later administration and proposed variation.', 'Redraft an ambiguous limitation and identify which enforceability questions drafting alone cannot settle.'])

add(26, 'Mistake, Misrepresentation, Duress and Undue Influence', 'commercial',
 'Research applicable Liberian rules and judgments on consent defects and remedies. The comparative video explains categories rather than supplying a local outcome.',
 'Mistake concerns a relevant erroneous belief; misrepresentation concerns a legally significant false statement inducing agreement. Duress involves improper pressure under the applicable test, while undue influence concerns abuse of an influential relationship.',
 'Begin with the asserted defect and facts proving it. Not every inaccurate prediction is a false statement of existing fact, and not every hard bargain is legally improper pressure. Identify the statement or conduct, its maker, the recipient’s knowledge, its effect on agreement and the applicable legal standard. Separate a party’s later disappointment from a defect present in formation.\n\nThe legal consequence depends on the recognised category and remedy. Rescission aims to unwind a transaction; damages address a legally recoverable loss. They must not be treated as interchangeable or automatic. Consider timing, affirmation, third-party interests and practical restoration under the governing rules. Distinguish honest disagreement about quality from an evidence-supported allegation of deception. Describe unproved fraud as an allegation, not an established criminal finding.',
 'Fictional case: a seller states that a workshop generator has recently been rebuilt, while an available repair record suggests no rebuild. The buyer also alleges pressure because delivery was urgently needed. A weak answer calls every urgent contract void. A strong answer identifies the factual representation, checks its accuracy and material effect, distinguishes commercial urgency from the applicable duress test, and evaluates possible remedies without promising either rescission or a fixed sum.',
 [('Misrepresentation','Legally significant false statement inducing agreement'),('Duress','Improper pressure assessed under the governing test'),('Rescission','Remedy seeking to unwind a transaction')],
 'Produce a consent-defects matrix for the generator sale, with one column each for elements, supporting facts, contrary facts, missing evidence and possible remedies.', [
 'What most directly supports investigating misrepresentation in the generator sale?|Evidence about the rebuild statement, its accuracy and reliance|The buyer’s disappointment alone|Any urgent need for delivery|The fact that the seller made a profit|A misrepresentation analysis requires a relevant statement and its effect, not mere dissatisfaction.',
 'Why is commercial urgency not automatically duress?|The governing test requires analysis of the nature and impropriety of pressure|Urgency proves every element of fraud|Only profitable contracts involve pressure|All hurried agreements are unenforceable|Practical need and legally improper pressure are distinct concepts.',
 'What distinguishes rescission from damages?|Unwinding a transaction differs from compensation for recoverable loss|Both always produce the same monetary award|Rescission is an automatic criminal conviction|Damages erase all third-party rights|Different remedies require their own legal and factual foundations.',
 'A buyer knew the true condition before signing. Why might that matter?|It may affect reliance, mistake and the alleged inducement|It makes every statement criminal|It removes the need to analyse any contract term|It guarantees the seller can exclude every duty|The recipient’s actual knowledge can change the formation-defect analysis.'
 ], 'ADrOhfHz13w',
 'Avoid a generic “unfair contract” conclusion. Identify the particular consent defect, test its elements and explain the conditions and limitations of the requested remedy.',
 ['Separate hard bargaining, a factual representation and an alleged threat in three fictional negotiations.', 'Argue rescission and compensation separately, identifying the evidence each proposed remedy needs.'])

add(27, 'Vicarious Liability, Occupiers and Nuisance', 'civil',
 'Find Liberian judgments relevant to civil responsibility and the particular claim. Use procedural and evidence provisions to organise proof; a foreign tort test must be checked against local authority.',
 'Direct liability concerns a person’s own legally wrongful conduct. Vicarious liability concerns responsibility for another’s conduct under a recognised relationship and connection test. Occupier and nuisance questions concern control of premises or interference with another’s legally protected use.',
 'Keep relationship, conduct and connection separate. Proof that someone works for a business does not alone determine whether the business is responsible for every act they perform. Identify the worker’s actual role, the assigned activity and the conduct causing the alleged harm, then research the governing connection test. A principal’s own negligent instructions may raise direct liability independently.\n\nPremises cases require attention to who controlled the relevant area, who encountered the risk and what precaution the applicable duty demanded. Nuisance analysis asks about the character and legal significance of an interference rather than treating every inconvenience as actionable. The claimant must connect the defendant to the relevant conduct and prove the legally required harm. Avoid collapsing a landowner, occupier, contractor and employee into one undifferentiated “responsible person”.',
 'Fictional case: a delivery driver damages a neighbour’s gate while using a company truck. The driver claims to have been delivering goods; the business says the trip was entirely personal. Separately, runoff from the warehouse repeatedly enters the neighbour’s yard. A weak answer makes the owner automatically liable for all losses. A strong answer investigates the trip’s purpose and authority, identifies who controlled the drainage, analyses the separate legal bases and avoids recovering the same loss twice.',
 [('Direct liability','Responsibility for one’s own legally wrongful conduct'),('Vicarious liability','Responsibility for another’s conduct under a recognised legal test'),('Control of premises','Relevant authority over a place or risk, distinct from bare ownership')],
 'Create a responsibility-and-evidence matrix for the truck and drainage incidents. Write a 900-word analysis separating direct, vicarious and interference claims.', [
 'What is essential beyond proving that a driver is employed by a business?|The governing relationship and connection between the wrongful act and the work|The company’s logo on any vehicle alone|The fact that the claimant prefers to sue the business|A rule that employers answer for every private act|Vicarious responsibility requires the legally relevant connection, not employment status alone.',
 'Why distinguish warehouse ownership from control of the drainage system?|The person legally responsible for the relevant risk must be identified|Only owners can ever be liable|Only tenants can ever be liable|Control is never relevant to a civil claim|Different roles may carry different duties and require separate evidence.',
 'Which fact most directly tests the business’s personal-trip argument?|The delivery assignment, route and purpose of the journey|The driver’s favourite vehicle|The neighbour’s opinion of the company|The truck’s purchase price alone|The factual connection to the business activity is central to the responsibility analysis.',
 'Why analyse repeated runoff separately from the damaged gate?|Different conduct, duties and evidence may support distinct causes of action|Every loss at one address has the same legal basis|A nuisance allegation automatically proves a vehicle collision|The claimant may collect identical damages twice|Separate incidents need separate elements analysis and a coherent loss account.'
 ], 'YjZTxAO6hGM',
 'Name each proposed defendant and legal basis. Link control, relationship and conduct to evidence before considering causation, defences and recoverable loss.',
 ['Draw a roles map distinguishing owner, occupier, contractor and driver.', 'Argue both versions of the truck journey using only the facts supported by the fictional delivery record.'])

add(27, 'Defamation, Privacy and Civil Damages', 'constitution',
 'Read the expression provisions of the Constitution and locate current Liberian authority on the specific civil claim. Verify any statutory change relevant to media or publication.',
 'Defamation concerns legally actionable injury to reputation through a communicated statement. Privacy concerns protection of personal information or interests under applicable law. Damages compensate legally recoverable loss and require a reasoned legal and evidential basis.',
 'Identify the exact words or image, who was referred to, who received the publication and what meaning is alleged. Distinguish a factual assertion from opinion, an allegation from a finding, and publication to others from a private statement. Then research the elements and available defences in the governing Liberian framework. Criticism is not automatically unlawful merely because it is embarrassing.\n\nTruth, protected occasions and other recognised defences require their own factual analysis; a disclaimer does not cure every publication. A privacy claim may address disclosure even where reputational falsity is absent, so it must not be treated as a duplicate defamation claim. For damages, separate proved financial loss, legally recognised non-financial harm and unsupported speculation. Preserve the original publication, date, audience evidence, responses and any correction. A larger demand is not stronger proof.',
 'Fictional case: a local page names a tailor as a thief after hearing a disputed customer complaint. It also publishes the tailor’s private medical document. A weak answer treats both acts as one proven crime. A strong civil analysis separates the words and their publication from the private document’s disclosure, investigates truth and applicable protection, and builds a supported loss schedule. It identifies which constitutional and civil questions need current local authority.',
 [('Defamation','Legally actionable injury to reputation through publication'),('Privacy','Protection of personal information or interests under applicable law'),('Loss schedule','Structured account of claimed harm and supporting evidence')],
 'Draft a balanced publication-risk memorandum and an evidence-based loss schedule for the tailor. Include an analysis of a correction and two plausible defences.', [
 'Why record the precise published words in a defamation analysis?|Meaning, identification and defences depend on what was actually communicated|A general description always proves every element|All criticism has identical legal effect|The claimant’s chosen damages amount determines the meaning|The actual publication is the starting point for analysing the alleged wrong.',
 'A private medical document is disclosed accurately. What issue may remain?|A distinct privacy or confidentiality question under applicable law|Falsity is automatically established|Accurate information can never involve legal protection|Every disclosure is a court order|Accuracy and lawful handling of private information are separate matters.',
 'What improves a claim for business loss after publication?|Records connecting the publication to legally recoverable financial harm|An unsupported round figure|A foreign damages award copied as a local entitlement|The number of exclamation marks in the complaint|Damages require an evidential connection and an applicable legal basis.',
 'A publisher claims a statement was opinion. What must be analysed?|The statement’s actual meaning, context and recognised local protection|Whether the word opinion appeared anywhere on the page|Whether opinion automatically defeats every claim|Whether an audience member disliked it|A defence must be examined against the particular words and governing law.'
 ], 'oJve0sPKSzE',
 'Separate each publication, cause of action and proposed defence. Explain how the record supports each element and avoid treating embarrassment as automatic proof of every claimed loss.',
 ['Compare a factual accusation, an attributed allegation and a stated opinion in three fictional posts.', 'Build a loss schedule distinguishing supported items from matters requiring further evidence.'])

add(28, 'Inchoate Offences: Attempt, Conspiracy and Participation', 'penal',
 'Read the Penal Law provisions on incomplete offences and participation, checking amendments and the precise elements of the alleged offence.',
 'An inchoate offence addresses conduct directed towards another offence before that target offence is completed. Attempt and conspiracy are distinct legal categories. Participation concerns a person’s legally relevant contribution, which must be proved individually.',
 'For attempt, distinguish intention, preparation and the conduct required by the governing statutory test. Failure to complete a target offence does not automatically eliminate criminal responsibility, but intention alone does not establish every inchoate offence. For conspiracy, identify the alleged agreement, its unlawful object and any further requirement imposed by the applicable law.\n\nAnalyse each accused separately. Presence, friendship or association is not a substitute for evidence of the required agreement, assistance or mental element. An abandoned plan, intervention or claimed withdrawal may raise a separate question that must be tested under the local rules rather than a foreign mnemonic. Build an elements chart using the actual statutory text, record evidence for and against each element, and distinguish the target offence from the alleged incomplete offence.',
 'Fictional case: two people discuss taking a locked generator from a workshop. One purchases ordinary tools, another later approaches the door, and a third is simply nearby. The weak answer convicts all three because they know each other. A strong analysis separates the alleged agreement, each person’s intention and acts, the statutory threshold for attempt, and the evidence of participation. It does not assume that buying tools alone is either sufficient or irrelevant under every possible legal test.',
 [('Preparation','Earlier planning or conduct before the legally relevant attempt threshold'),('Attempt','Incomplete offending meeting the applicable conduct and mental requirements'),('Conspiracy','Agreement-based offence as defined by governing law')],
 'Produce separate elements-and-evidence charts for the three people. Write a 700-word comparison of the strongest prosecution and defence arguments for each.', [
 'Why does an unsuccessful plan still require legal analysis?|An incomplete offence may exist if its statutory elements are proved|Failure to complete the target offence always eliminates liability|Discussion alone proves every criminal charge|All incomplete conduct has one universal legal test|Attempt and conspiracy have distinct requirements that do not depend solely on completion.',
 'Which method avoids collective guilt by association?|Analyse each person’s acts, knowledge and required mental element|Assign the same role to everyone at the scene|Treat friendship as proof of agreement|Ignore evidence favourable to a bystander|Criminal responsibility must be supported for the individual accused.',
 'What distinguishes an attempt question from an agreement-based conspiracy question?|The statutory conduct threshold differs from proof of the required agreement|Both are proved only by a completed theft|An attempt always requires three people|A conspiracy can never involve intention|The categories require different elements and evidence.',
 'A person claims to have withdrawn from a plan. What is required?|Examine the relevant local rule, timing and acts supporting withdrawal|Assume any later denial is a complete defence|Ignore withdrawal because the plan was discussed|Apply a foreign rule without checking its status|The legal effect of withdrawal depends on the applicable framework and facts.'
 ], 'wt6Rfpyz1hA',
 'Start with the named statutory offence, distinguish preparation from the required conduct and address each accused individually. Avoid substituting suspicion for an elements analysis.',
 ['Place fictional acts on a timeline and identify the facts relevant to the statutory attempt threshold.', 'Draft opposing arguments about whether the record supports a particular alleged agreement.'])

add(28, 'Property Offences, Dishonesty and Proof', 'penal',
 'Locate the Penal Law provisions governing the alleged taking, deception or entrusted-property offence. Verify the wording rather than importing foreign offence labels.',
 'Property offences differ according to conduct, entitlement, consent, mental elements and any additional circumstances. An entrusted-property allegation differs from an unauthorised taking. A failed commercial promise is not automatically a proved criminal deception.',
 'Identify the property, the person with the relevant interest and the accused’s role. Ask how possession was obtained, what authority or consent existed, what conduct changed that position and which mental element the statute requires. Ownership, custody and authority to use an asset are different matters. An accounting shortfall is a fact to investigate, not a complete offence definition.\n\nWhere deception is alleged, identify the actual representation, its falsity and the causal role it played. A broken promise may support a civil issue without proving dishonest intention at the legally relevant time. Where force or threats are alleged, examine the specific offence and additional required facts. Trace records, permission, movement of funds and competing explanations. Keep the criminal burden of proof distinct from a business’s internal disciplinary conclusion.',
 'Fictional case: a cooperative treasurer transfers money from a fund to a supplier, and equipment never arrives. Members accuse the treasurer of stealing. The weak answer equates loss with guilt. The strong answer checks the treasurer’s authority, supplier agreement, bank trail, knowledge and alleged personal benefit. It separates possible breach, fraud by another party, error and any evidence-supported entrusted-property offence before stating a conditional conclusion.',
 [('Custody','Holding an asset, distinct from ownership or permission to use it'),('Consent or authority','Legally relevant permission whose scope must be established'),('Criminal proof','Proof of the statutory elements under the required burden')],
 'Prepare a financial-record request list and a 900-word elements analysis for the cooperative. Explain which facts would distinguish civil non-performance from an alleged offence.', [
 'Why is a financial loss not sufficient by itself to prove a property offence?|The statutory conduct and mental elements still require evidence|Every failed purchase is theft|The complainant’s allegation fixes the offence|A missing item removes the presumption of innocence|Loss may have several explanations and does not substitute for offence elements.',
 'What matters when a person originally received property lawfully?|The scope of their authority and the later alleged misuse|A rule that lawful receipt prevents every possible offence|A rule that all custody proves ownership|The asset’s price alone|Entrusted property requires analysis of permission, subsequent conduct and the governing offence.',
 'A supplier’s promise later fails. What must be checked before alleging criminal deception?|The relevant representation, mental element and evidence at the legally relevant time|The failure alone as conclusive criminal proof|Whether the buyer would prefer a criminal process|Whether the supplier owns a business card|Civil non-performance and criminal deception are distinct questions.',
 'Which record most directly helps test the treasurer’s alleged personal diversion?|The authorisation, bank trail and identity of the recipient|An unrelated cooperative meeting photograph|A general complaint about expensive equipment|The member count alone|A traceable transaction record can support or undermine the alleged misuse.'
 ], 'v6rWRBmhYZM',
 'Use the actual statutory elements as headings. Separate loss from wrongful conduct, and address authority, consent, mental state and alternative explanations with record-based reasoning.',
 ['Trace a fictional cooperative payment from authorisation to recipient and identify unresolved steps.', 'Compare a civil delivery dispute with an evidence-supported alleged deception using an elements table.'])

add(29, 'Charging, Disclosure and Pre-Trial Applications', 'criminal-procedure',
 'Read the Criminal Procedure Law with Article 21 and applicable court rules. Identify the current charging and pre-trial procedures for the proposed offence.',
 'A charge states an alleged offence through the applicable process; it is not a finding of guilt. Disclosure concerns access to legally required material. Pre-trial applications address issues that may need determination before the substantive hearing.',
 'Read the charging document for the alleged conduct, date, parties and offence particulars required by law. Distinguish a police narrative, an indictment or other authorised charging instrument, and a judicial determination. Any defect must be analysed against the governing requirements and available remedy rather than assumed to end the case automatically.\n\nCreate a disclosure and evidence plan identifying statements, records, exhibits and potentially favourable material. Availability of an item does not establish admissibility or reliability. A pre-trial application should name its legal issue, requested order and supporting record; it should not ask the court to accept an unsupported account as the final verdict. Check local procedure for objections, notice, counsel and access to material. Do not import a foreign prosecution form or discovery timetable into Liberia.',
 'Fictional case: an accused is charged over a missing warehouse item. The investigator’s summary mentions a stock count but omits a witness statement placing the accused elsewhere. A weak response demands instant acquittal without examining the record. A strong response identifies the charge’s required particulars, requests the relevant complete material through lawful procedure, preserves the potentially favourable statement and explains the specific pre-trial issue requiring decision.',
 [('Charge','Formal allegation through the applicable process'),('Disclosure','Access to material required by governing procedural duties'),('Pre-trial application','Request to resolve a defined issue before substantive determination')],
 'Prepare a mock pre-trial file: charge checklist, disclosure request outline, favourable-evidence note and a concise application for a specified lawful order.', [
 'What does a valid charging document establish?|The formal allegation to be determined through lawful proceedings|A final conviction|Conclusive reliability of every witness|Automatic defeat of all defences|A charge defines the allegation rather than proving its truth.',
 'Why identify a potentially favourable omitted statement?|It may affect preparation, proof and relevant procedural duties|It automatically makes every other record inadmissible|It authorises editing the investigator’s original file|It guarantees a particular verdict|Relevant contrary material should be investigated through the applicable process.',
 'What makes a pre-trial application legally focused?|A defined issue, governing authority, supporting record and requested order|A repetition of every accusation with no requested relief|A foreign form presented as local law|A demand to skip the entire evidential process|A precise application helps the court identify what it is lawfully being asked to decide.',
 'A learner finds an error in a charge. What must be analysed next?|The applicable requirement, effect of the defect and available procedural remedy|Automatic termination under every legal system|Automatic conviction despite the error|Whether the learner can privately correct the filed original|Not every defect has the same legal consequence or procedural response.'
 ], 'ED9njYaF75w',
 'Distinguish charging sufficiency, disclosure duties and the requested pre-trial decision. Connect each issue to local authority and a specific part of the record.',
 ['Identify allegations, evidence descriptions and conclusions in a fictional investigator’s summary.', 'Draft a focused disclosure request that explains the relevance of each requested record.'])

add(29, 'Criminal Appeals, Review and Wrongful-Conviction Safeguards', 'criminal-procedure',
 'Read the local provisions and court rules on criminal appeals and review with constitutional protections. Verify the available route, record requirements and current deadlines.',
 'An appeal tests an identified decision through an authorised review process. Grounds may concern law, procedure or another recognised review issue. A claim of wrongful conviction requires careful handling of the judgment, record and evidence rather than a public assertion alone.',
 'Begin with the judgment and the rule governing review. Identify the alleged error, where it appears in the record and how it could affect the outcome under the applicable standard. An appeal is not automatically a fresh trial with unrestricted new evidence. Separate disagreement with a witness from a legally recognised ground for intervention.\n\nA responsible review file records preservation of issues, relevant rulings, exhibits and any missing transcript. If new material appears, determine the proper procedural route and how its origin and reliability can be assessed. Avoid promising release merely because an inconsistency exists. Protect the dignity and confidential information of accused persons and witnesses, and distinguish an allegation of error from a final appellate determination. Legal assistance must be provided by authorised persons within the applicable rules.',
 'Fictional case: a learner reads a public conviction judgment and notices that an admitted exhibit appears inconsistent with the court’s account of a key date. A weak commentary declares innocence immediately. A strong review note obtains the complete relevant record, identifies the possible factual or legal error and its materiality, considers whether the issue was raised, and researches the correct local review route. The note states that an inconsistency requires investigation rather than proving a remedy automatically.',
 [('Ground of appeal','Recognised basis for challenging a decision'),('Materiality','Significance of an alleged error to the outcome'),('Review record','Relevant documents, evidence and rulings supporting the challenge')],
 'Write a 1,000-word mock appellate issues memorandum using a public decision or a fictional record. Include grounds, record pinpoints, counterarguments and the procedural questions to verify.', [
 'Which product best supports a criminal appeal issue?|A record-based ground identifying the alleged error and its possible material effect|An unsupported declaration of innocence|A new accusation against every witness|A demand that all appeals become fresh trials|Review requires a recognised ground connected to the decision and governing standard.',
 'Why check whether an issue was raised in the original proceeding?|Preservation and available review routes may affect how it can be considered|An unraised issue proves the accused is guilty|All errors become irrelevant once judgment is entered|Preservation rules are identical worldwide|The procedural history can affect the lawful review analysis.',
 'New evidence appears after judgment. What should be examined?|Its provenance, reliability and the authorised route for presenting it|Whether social-media circulation makes it admissible|Whether a learner can insert it into the old record privately|Whether any new item automatically reverses conviction|New material must be handled through the appropriate process and evidential safeguards.',
 'Why distinguish an inconsistency from a guaranteed appellate remedy?|Its legal significance and effect must be tested under the governing review standard|All inconsistencies prove innocence|No inconsistency can ever matter|An appeal’s outcome is controlled by public opinion|A possible error is the beginning of analysis rather than its completed result.'
 ], '0YyLkywhIx8',
 'State a precise proposed ground, cite the record and explain materiality under the local review framework. Identify which remedial conclusion depends on further record or authority.',
 ['Convert three vague complaints about a trial into possible grounds tied to a fictional record.', 'Prepare a provenance checklist for a document discovered after judgment.'])

add(30, 'Witness Examination, Expert Evidence and Credibility', 'civil',
 'Read the evidence provisions and relevant Liberian rulings on witness competence, opinions and examination. Verify the applicable rule for the particular proceeding.',
 'A factual witness gives evidence within legally permitted knowledge; an expert offers specialised opinion within the governing rules. Credibility concerns reliability and truthfulness. The weight of an opinion depends on its basis, method and fit to the issue, not credentials alone.',
 'Begin with what the witness can properly address. Direct observation, inference and reported information require different treatment. A factual witness should not be turned into an expert merely because a question asks for a technical conclusion. For an expert, investigate qualifications, the material reviewed, assumptions, method and limits. Identify whether the opinion answers a relevant issue and whether its legal admission conditions are met.\n\nExamination should expose the basis of the account rather than reward confidence or dramatic presentation. Compare contemporaneous records and competing explanations without assuming every inconsistency is deliberate dishonesty. Cross-examination can test knowledge, method and assumptions, but questions are not evidence unless the governing process makes the resulting answer admissible. Separate admissibility from the persuasive weight the fact-finder may assign after hearing the evidence.',
 'Fictional case: a road-incident claim includes an engineer’s report based on a photograph taken after repairs, and a passenger’s direct account of the collision. A weak answer accepts the engineer’s conclusion as final because of the job title. A strong analysis identifies the engineer’s assumptions and missing measurements, separates those limits from the passenger’s opportunity to observe, and prepares targeted questions about both accounts. It does not substitute the learner’s personal guess for technical evidence.',
 [('Expert opinion','Specialised assessment within the relevant rules and expertise'),('Credibility','Reliability and truthfulness assessed from relevant evidence'),('Weight','Persuasive value after considering basis, limits and competing proof')],
 'Create a witness matrix and ten focused examination questions for the passenger and engineer. Add an 800-word note separating admission issues from weight.', [
 'Why is an expert’s professional title not sufficient by itself?|The opinion’s relevant basis, method and limits must also be examined|Experts never provide useful evidence|Credentials automatically decide the legal issue|A technical opinion replaces every factual record|Expertise must be connected to a supported opinion relevant to the dispute.',
 'Which question directly tests the engineer’s assumptions?|“What condition of the road existed when the photograph was taken?”|“Do you prefer this claimant?”|“Would you agree the court should always accept experts?”|“Is your report printed professionally?”|The timing and condition of the source material may affect the opinion’s reliability.',
 'What distinguishes an admissibility decision from evidential weight?|Permission to receive evidence differs from how persuasive it is|Every admitted item is conclusive|Weak evidence is automatically a constitutional amendment|Weight determines the court’s original jurisdiction|An admitted item may still have substantial limitations in proving a fact.',
 'A witness has one inconsistent date. What is the proper response?|Examine its cause, materiality and relationship to the rest of the record|Assume every part of the account is knowingly false|Ignore it because the witness is confident|Treat the questioner’s accusation as proved evidence|Credibility should be assessed proportionately from the whole relevant record.'
 ], 'KBRXOv1laRw',
 'Identify the witness’s proper subject, the governing admission rule and the basis for assessing weight. Tie each proposed examination question to a specific factual or methodological issue.',
 ['Label a report’s observations, assumptions and opinions in separate columns.', 'Practise a short examination that tests the photograph’s provenance and the passenger’s opportunity to observe.'])

add(30, 'Privilege, Improperly Obtained Evidence and Objections', 'ethics',
 'Read the Liberian ethics code, constitutional safeguards and evidence rules relevant to the issue. Distinguish ethical confidentiality from any legally recognised privilege or exclusion rule.',
 'Privilege can protect specified communications or information from compelled disclosure under its conditions. Confidentiality is a broader duty concerning information handling. An objection asks the court to determine a defined legal problem with offered evidence or procedure.',
 'Identify who holds a claimed protection, the relationship in which the communication arose, its purpose and any relevant disclosure or exception. Merely labelling a document “confidential” does not settle legal privilege. Equally, an ethical duty may persist even where a particular evidence rule does not prevent disclosure. Assess the applicable Liberian framework rather than copying a foreign privilege test.\n\nWhere evidence was allegedly obtained improperly, separate the method of obtaining it, constitutional or statutory rights and the legal consequence for its use. A violation may raise remedies or accountability without producing the same exclusion rule in every jurisdiction. An objection should specify the item, rule, reason and requested ruling; argumentative outrage is not a substitute. Preserve the objection and decision as required for review. Never recommend secretly obtaining protected information to improve a classroom case.',
 'Fictional case: a former assistant shares a legal-advice email and an unrelated public invoice from an office file. A weak response treats everything in the folder as either automatically privileged or automatically usable. A strong response analyses each item, its purpose, who holds any protection and how disclosure occurred; examines separate confidentiality and evidential questions; and identifies a lawful objection or referral rather than exploiting potentially protected material.',
 [('Privilege','Conditional legal protection against compelled disclosure'),('Confidentiality','Duty concerning handling and disclosure of information'),('Objection','Focused request for a ruling on a legal evidence or procedure issue')],
 'Write a privilege-and-objection memorandum for the two documents. Include an ethical handling plan and explain why their location in one file does not settle their status.', [
 'Why does a “confidential” label not by itself establish legal privilege?|The governing protection has conditions that must be proved|All labels automatically create judicial immunity|No confidential information can ever be protected|Public invoices always become legal advice in an office file|The legal basis and communication’s function determine the privilege analysis.',
 'How should two different documents from the same folder be assessed?|Individually according to their purpose, source and applicable protections|As legally identical because their physical location is shared|As both admissible whenever an assistant releases them|As both permanently excluded without further inquiry|Storage location alone does not determine legal status.',
 'What makes an evidential objection properly focused?|Identify the item, applicable rule, basis and requested ruling|Accuse every witness of dishonesty|Insist all improperly obtained evidence has one worldwide rule|Replace legal grounds with indignation|A court needs a defined legal question and requested response.',
 'Why separate obtaining evidence improperly from its admissibility?|The right violated and the governing evidential consequence require distinct analysis|Improper obtaining always proves the underlying claim|Every legal system has identical exclusion rules|An ethical concern makes jurisdiction unnecessary|Legal consequences depend on the particular rights, rules and remedies.'
 ], 'eFzzBg-Faho',
 'Analyse each protection and item separately. Explain the distinction between an ethical handling duty, an admissibility question and an available remedy for improper conduct.',
 ['Sort fictional communications into public information, confidential information and items requiring a privilege analysis.', 'Practise a concise objection that states its legal basis without revealing unnecessary protected content.'])

add(31, 'Leases, Easements and Co-Ownership', 'land',
 'Read the Land Rights Act and relevant Liberian property and civil authorities. Determine which locally recognised interest or permission is asserted before borrowing foreign estate terminology.',
 'A lease grants a legally defined right to use or possess property under its terms. An easement concerns a recognised use right over another property. Co-ownership involves shared legally recognised interests, whose powers and obligations require their own analysis.',
 'Distinguish title, possession and permitted use. A tenant’s physical control does not make them the owner, and a licence to cross land is not automatically an easement or transfer. Identify the person granting the right, their authority, the land category, form and scope of the arrangement, and any legally required process.\n\nFor a lease, examine parties, premises, duration, rent, repair responsibilities, termination and dispute provisions. For an access right, establish location, purpose, users and conditions instead of assuming unrestricted use. Where interests are shared, determine the legal form, consent required for dealing with the asset and responsibility for income, expenses and disputes. A foreign category such as a particular concurrent estate must be tied to local recognition before it becomes the rule in a Liberian answer.',
 'Fictional case: two relatives share an inherited building. One grants a five-year workshop lease and promises the tenant unlimited access across a neighbour’s plot. A weak answer assumes the signature binds every owner and neighbour. A strong answer checks the signer’s interest and authority, the co-owners’ rights, the lease terms and the independent legal basis for access. It separates use of the building from a claimed right over someone else’s land.',
 [('Lease','Defined right of use or possession under a lawful arrangement'),('Easement','Recognised use right affecting another property'),('Co-ownership','Shared interests whose powers depend on the governing form')],
 'Prepare a lease-and-access term sheet and an 800-word authority memorandum. Identify the documents needed before assuming the arrangement binds co-owners or neighbours.', [
 'A tenant occupies a building under a lease. What must not be assumed?|Possession alone proves ownership of the building|Possession may arise under agreed terms|The lease needs an identifiable landlord|The permitted use may have conditions|Possession, title and authority to grant further rights are distinct.',
 'Why analyse a promised path over neighbouring land separately?|The landlord needs a lawful basis for granting that independent use right|A lease automatically creates rights over every neighbouring plot|All access permission transfers land ownership|The tenant’s need determines the neighbour’s legal obligations|An agreement about one property does not establish authority over another.',
 'One co-owner signs a long lease. What must be checked?|Their powers and any legally required consent of other interest holders|Whether one signature always binds all co-owners|Whether shared ownership eliminates every lease|Whether the tenant likes the premises|Authority depends on the recognised ownership arrangement and applicable rules.',
 'Why identify the scope of a land-use permission?|Its location, purpose, users and conditions determine the asserted right|All permissions authorise any future use|Every permission is a perpetual transfer|Scope matters only after a court refuses every claim|The precise legal interest and permitted conduct must be established.'
 ], 'TtuepIM8UM4',
 'Use separate headings for title, authority and each asserted use right. Explain which local legal form supports the arrangement and where further recognition or consent must be verified.',
 ['Draw a map of the building, neighbour’s plot and asserted access route, marking distinct rights.', 'Negotiate a fictional lease checklist while maintaining a separate list of unresolved title and authority questions.'])

add(31, 'Land Transactions, Surveys and Community Consent', 'land',
 'Read the Land Rights Act and Liberia Land Authority guidance on document vetting and land services. For customary land, verify the applicable community governance and participation requirements.',
 'A survey describes and locates land; it is not by itself proof of title or authority to transfer. Due diligence investigates rights, boundaries, restrictions and competing claims. Community consent requires the process and representation recognised by applicable law.',
 'Check the seller’s identity and legal interest, the land category, source documents and any overlapping rights. Reconcile a survey plan with the physical site and document descriptions; a precise drawing of disputed land does not settle who may convey it. Verify whether the purported representative has authority and whether required community decisions and participation occurred.\n\nA transaction file should distinguish completed checks from assurances still awaiting evidence. Record the original source, authenticity questions, public or institutional checks, boundary observations and unresolved claims. Check the separate role of planning, environmental or other approvals rather than treating land permission as universal operational approval. Document gaps before commitment and avoid promising “guaranteed title” because one intermediary produced a deed. Use qualified local professionals for real transactions.',
 'Fictional case: an investor proposes a training centre near a customary community and receives a survey plus a letter signed by one spokesperson. Several residents dispute the boundary and say no community decision occurred. A weak answer concludes that the professional-looking plan proves a valid sale. A strong answer verifies the land category, community representation, actual participation, overlapping claims and source documents, while separating the proposed centre’s regulatory permissions from land rights.',
 [('Survey','Description and location of land, distinct from title'),('Due diligence','Documented investigation of rights, restrictions and risk'),('Community authority','Legally recognised power and process to act for the community')],
 'Build a 12-item transaction checklist and a stop/go issues register for the training centre. Assign a legal or factual reason to each unresolved check.', [
 'What does a survey plan establish without further title investigation?|A description or location that still needs to be linked to lawful rights|Conclusive ownership in every circumstance|Authority of any spokesperson to sell|Automatic planning and environmental approval|A survey and lawful title address different questions.',
 'Why investigate the spokesperson’s mandate?|Community representation and required participation must have a lawful basis|One signature always proves every resident consented|Customary land has no legal safeguards|An investor can choose any representative to bind the community|The legitimacy of authority cannot be inferred solely from a signed letter.',
 'A boundary is disputed despite matching document names. What should happen?|Investigate the overlapping claims and reconcile records with the site|Ignore the dispute because names match|Alter the survey to remove the neighbour|Promise that a course certificate validates the transfer|Document consistency does not eliminate a competing-rights issue.',
 'Why keep operational approvals separate from land permission?|Different legal authorisations address different rights and regulatory purposes|Land permission automatically licenses all activities|Environmental questions determine every ownership issue|A deed replaces every statutory process|Holding a land right does not by itself establish compliance for the proposed operation.'
 ], 'g1JA01KRXkc',
 'Treat survey, title, authority, participation and regulatory permissions as distinct checks. Explain how each missing item changes the transaction risk rather than issuing a blanket guarantee.',
 ['Compare a fictional survey, deed description and boundary sketch to identify inconsistencies.', 'Simulate a community-information meeting that records questions and unresolved representation issues without manufacturing consent.'])

add(32, 'Wills, Intestacy and Administration of Estates', 'estates',
 'Read the Decedents Estates Law, Title 8, including the provisions relevant to testamentary and intestate administration. Check amendments and applicable protections before applying any distribution rule.',
 'A will is a testamentary instrument whose validity depends on governing requirements. Intestacy concerns property passing without an effective testamentary disposition. Estate administration gathers assets, resolves obligations and distributes according to lawful authority.',
 'Distinguish making a will, proving it through the relevant process and acting as an authorised representative. The existence of a document headed “will” does not establish capacity, execution or the scope of its effect. Identify the property the decedent could dispose of and any legally relevant claims or restrictions. A relative’s expectation is different from a verified entitlement.\n\nAn estate file needs an inventory, liabilities record, title questions, the claimed testamentary documents and the representative’s authority. Do not distribute disputed property merely because a family meeting supports a particular outcome. Intestate shares, priority of representatives, notice and court procedure come from current Liberian law, not a foreign video. Explain which issue can be analysed now and which requires a probate ruling, verified record or authorised professional assistance.',
 'Fictional case: a family in Greenville finds two documents said to be wills, one unsigned, while the decedent’s land ownership and a lender’s claim are disputed. A weak answer divides everything equally by personal preference. A strong answer identifies the competing testamentary instruments, the execution and capacity questions, the property actually in the estate, outstanding obligations and the need for lawful administration. It avoids inventing local inheritance fractions.',
 [('Testate disposition','Transfer directed by an effective testamentary instrument'),('Intestacy','Passing of property without an effective testamentary disposition'),('Estate administration','Lawful collection, management and distribution of the estate')],
 'Prepare an estate inventory and a 1,000-word issues memorandum for the family. Separate will validity, ownership, liabilities, representation and distribution questions.', [
 'Why does the heading “will” not settle validity?|Execution, capacity and other governing requirements still need examination|All handwritten documents are automatically valid wills|Every will must be treated as intestacy|Family agreement alone replaces all legal requirements|Testamentary status depends on the applicable law and relevant facts.',
 'Which item must be resolved before distributing claimed land?|Whether and to what extent the property belongs to the estate|Only which relative arrives first|Only whether the land is valuable|Only whether a foreign video shows equal shares|Administration cannot assume ownership of disputed assets.',
 'What distinguishes a representative’s authority from a family member’s expectation?|A lawful appointment or recognised basis is needed to administer assets|Every relative may dispose of every asset immediately|Expectation automatically creates representative powers|An inventory alone appoints an administrator|Administration and beneficial entitlement are separate legal questions.',
 'Why must a Liberian intestacy problem use local distribution rules?|Entitlements and procedures depend on the governing Liberian law|Inheritance fractions are identical worldwide|A comparative video is a Liberian court order|Personal preference may replace statutory provisions|Comparative examples do not establish local succession entitlements.'
 ], '18l1ejgXtBU',
 'Organise the answer around instrument validity, estate property, obligations, representative authority and distribution. Use the local statute for the exact rule instead of inventing fractions.',
 ['Classify fictional assets as established estate property, disputed title or property requiring further documentation.', 'Build a probate-file checklist that keeps representative authority separate from proposed beneficiary shares.'])

add(32, 'Trusts, Fiduciary Administration and Beneficiary Protection', 'estates',
 'Use the Decedents Estates Law and verified Liberian authority relevant to the proposed arrangement. Check whether the legal relationship and trustee powers are recognised before assuming a foreign trust model applies.',
 'A trust separates administration of property from beneficial interests under a recognised legal arrangement. A fiduciary must act within the governing duties and purpose, accounting for decisions and handling conflicts. A beneficiary’s interest differs from an administrator’s personal ownership.',
 'Identify the instrument or other legal basis, the property, beneficiaries, purpose and powers. A promise to “look after money” is not enough to establish every element of a legally recognised trust. Distinguish a trustee’s administrative discretion from unrestricted personal use. Decisions should connect to authorised purposes and be supported by records.\n\nInvestigate duties of loyalty, care, accounting and lawful distribution within the applicable framework. A conflict is not cured merely because a transaction appears profitable. Keep separate records for property administered for others, identify fees or expenses requiring authorisation and record information owed to beneficiaries. Available relief for breach depends on the duty, conduct, loss and procedure; it is not automatically a fixed monetary award or criminal conviction.',
 'Fictional case: an adult is appointed to manage a fund for two children’s education. They lend part of it to their own business and later say the arrangement may earn a profit. A weak answer treats potential profit as complete justification. A strong answer checks the governing instrument, investment powers, conflict rules and accounting records, considers the beneficiaries’ protection and distinguishes authorised administration from an unapproved personal benefit.',
 [('Legal administration','Authority to manage property within governing powers'),('Beneficial interest','Interest held for the person protected by the arrangement'),('Fiduciary conflict','Personal or competing interest affecting a duty owed to another')],
 'Write a fiduciary-accounting checklist and an 800-word conflict-and-remedies memorandum. Identify both records required and legal questions that remain unresolved.', [
 'Why does possible profit not itself justify a trustee’s loan to their own business?|Authority and conflict duties must be analysed independently of the hoped-for return|All profitable transactions automatically satisfy fiduciary duties|Beneficiaries have no interest in administration|A business loan can never involve a conflict|Fiduciary responsibility concerns lawful purpose, powers and loyalty as well as financial result.',
 'What must be established before applying a foreign trust model locally?|The recognised Liberian legal basis and governing arrangement|Only whether the document uses the word trust|Only whether the administrator is respected|A rule that every promise creates an identical trust|Legal recognition and the instrument’s terms determine the relationship.',
 'Which record supports accountable fiduciary administration?|Separate asset, decision, income and expenditure records|A single personal account with no transaction explanations|Only verbal assurances to one relative|Only the administrator’s projected profit|Beneficiaries and lawful oversight need a traceable administration record.',
 'An alleged breach is identified. What remains necessary for a remedy analysis?|The duty, conduct, consequences and available procedure|Automatic criminal guilt without proof|A universal fixed payment to every beneficiary|Immediate private seizure of all the administrator’s assets|A remedy must match the established breach and governing legal framework.'
 ], 'PGVKADO4Qhs',
 'Establish the arrangement, identify its powers and duties, apply them to the conflicted act and explain possible relief with evidential and procedural limits.',
 ['Audit a fictional education fund’s ledger for missing authorisation and mixing of property.', 'Draft beneficiary questions that seek an account without prematurely alleging a proved offence.'])

add(33, 'Wages, Working Time, Leave and Occupational Safety', 'labour',
 'Read the Decent Work Act provisions relevant to remuneration, working time, leave and safety. Verify current regulations and applicability; foreign rates or hour limits are not Liberian rules.',
 'Remuneration terms describe what work is paid and how; working-time and leave rules regulate conditions under the applicable framework. Occupational safety requires analysis of risks, duties and protective measures in the actual workplace.',
 'Separate contractual promises from statutory minimum protections and identify which law covers the relationship. A pay analysis should reconcile agreed wages, hours or output, authorised deductions, payment records and the legal basis for any extra work. Gross pay, net pay and outstanding entitlement are different figures. Do not infer a current local rate from an old foreign lecture.\n\nFor leave, identify the qualifying circumstances, notice or evidence requirements and relevant entitlement rather than assuming every absence is misconduct. For safety, describe the hazard, exposed persons, feasible control measures and allocation of responsibilities. Protective equipment is one measure and does not necessarily remove a preventable hazard at its source. Preserve incident and medical records lawfully, protect privacy and separate a safety complaint from a concluded compensation claim.',
 'Fictional case: a small workshop records a machine operator as absent during a medical visit, deducts pay and requires unguarded machinery work afterward. A weak answer discusses wages only. A strong answer checks the employment terms, time and pay records, leave rules, medical evidence and safety duties separately. It identifies immediate risk controls and a lawful dispute route without promising a specific compensation amount.',
 [('Gross and net pay','Pay before and after legally relevant deductions'),('Leave entitlement','Protected absence subject to the applicable conditions'),('Risk control','Measure reducing exposure to a workplace hazard')],
 'Produce a pay-reconciliation table, leave-issues note and workplace-risk checklist for the operator. Explain which current provisions must be checked for each claimed entitlement.', [
 'What distinguishes gross from net pay in a wage dispute?|The legal and recorded basis of deductions must be reconciled|Net pay always equals every promised gross amount|Any deduction requested by an employer is automatically lawful|A payroll heading establishes the applicable statutory rate|Accurate analysis separates promised remuneration, deductions and actual payment.',
 'Why investigate a medical absence separately from misconduct?|The applicable leave conditions and evidence may change its legal treatment|Every medical absence automatically defeats all discipline|Medical records need not be handled confidentially|A payroll deduction proves criminal wrongdoing|Leave rights and disciplinary allegations require their own rules and facts.',
 'What is a stronger safety plan than providing equipment alone?|Identify and control the actual hazard and residual risk|Assume protective equipment removes every employer duty|Treat a signed waiver as universal permission for unsafe work|Wait for a judgment before recording an obvious hazard|Safety analysis considers the source of risk and effective controls under the governing duties.',
 'Which source should determine an asserted Liberian wage or working-time requirement?|The current applicable Liberian law and authorised regulations|A foreign training video’s numeric example|The worker’s preferred rate alone|A general social-media statement|Local applicability and current legal status must be established.'
 ], 'UW-omPizIt0',
 'Use separate headings for remuneration, leave and safety. Reconcile the records and apply the correct local requirements instead of importing rates or treating every issue as one grievance.',
 ['Reconcile a fictional timesheet with wages, deductions and payments, marking unresolved differences.', 'Rank workplace hazards by the controls required and identify records supporting a lawful safety complaint.'])

add(33, 'Trade Unions, Collective Bargaining and Labour Institutions', 'labour',
 'Read the Decent Work Act provisions relevant to association, collective relations and labour dispute institutions. Verify the applicable recognition and dispute procedures.',
 'Collective bargaining concerns negotiation through legally relevant worker and employer representation. A collective agreement differs from an individual employment contract. Labour institutions may have specialised powers that must be distinguished from informal negotiation.',
 'Identify the represented group, the basis of representation, the issues open for bargaining and any required recognition or procedure. A representative’s mandate matters: an agreement cannot be assumed to bind everyone merely because a meeting occurred. Distinguish proposing terms, reaching agreement and making those terms legally effective under the relevant framework.\n\nCollective terms interact with individual contracts and statutory protections. An individual worker’s preference does not alone decide the legal effect of a collective instrument, and negotiation does not authorise departure from non-waivable rights. Map grievance stages, specialised institutions and available review, using the current local statute. Examine the lawful conditions governing industrial action rather than treating every work stoppage as automatically lawful or unlawful. Keep retaliation allegations tied to evidence and the applicable protections.',
 'Fictional case: workers at a transport cooperative appoint two spokespersons to discuss rosters. Management says their meeting proves all workers surrendered future leave claims. A weak answer accepts either side’s blanket claim. A strong analysis checks the representatives’ mandate, the actual agreed text, applicable statutory rights and the process giving an instrument legal effect. It prepares a grievance route and distinguishes a proposal from a binding agreement.',
 [('Collective bargaining','Negotiation through legally relevant representation'),('Collective agreement','Instrument with the legal effect given by its terms and governing law'),('Representation mandate','Authority to negotiate or act for a defined group')],
 'Draft a bargaining agenda, mandate statement and grievance-process map. Write a 700-word analysis of the claimed surrender of leave rights.', [
 'Why examine the spokespersons’ mandate?|Authority to represent and bind a defined group must be established|Any meeting authorises surrender of every right|Every individual preference overrides all collective rules|A mandate replaces every statutory requirement|Representation and the effect of an agreement require legal and factual support.',
 'What separates proposed roster terms from an effective collective instrument?|Agreement and any applicable recognition or formal requirements|A proposal’s page count alone|A manager’s belief that discussions ended|The attendance of one worker|Negotiation status must be distinguished from legal effectiveness.',
 'Why investigate statutory leave protections before accepting a waiver?|Some protections may limit what can lawfully be negotiated or waived|Every proposed waiver is valid if management wants it|All collective agreements automatically repeal legislation|Leave is never a legal issue|The governing statute constrains the effect of contractual and collective terms.',
 'How should the legality of a work stoppage be assessed?|Use the applicable local conditions, process and relevant facts|Treat every stoppage as identical across countries|Use a foreign news clip as a binding rule|Assume every union proposal is an adjudicated order|Industrial action requires its own jurisdiction-specific legal analysis.'
 ], 'p1CTPyfRA7M',
 'Separate mandate, negotiation status, legal effect and dispute route. Explain which statutory protections qualify the parties’ bargaining choices.',
 ['Role-play a bargaining session using a written mandate and clearly recorded provisional terms.', 'Compare an individual complaint, collective grievance and specialised institutional decision on a process chart.'])

add(34, 'Company Formation, Directors and Corporate Compliance', 'business',
 'Locate the Associations Law and amendments in the Liberian Registry library. Distinguish incorporation from applicable domestic business, licensing, tax and sector requirements.',
 'Incorporation creates a recognised entity through the governing process. Directors exercise powers subject to duties and the organisation’s lawful framework. Corporate compliance concerns obligations beyond simply possessing incorporation documents.',
 'Identify the entity form, constituent documents, registers and allocation of decision-making powers. A founder’s title does not necessarily authorise every transaction after incorporation. Distinguish board decisions, officer authority and shareholder matters by the governing rules and documents. Record who approved an act, any conflict and the basis for the decision.\n\nDuties and conflicts require analysis of the particular role and act. An interested director’s transaction cannot be assessed only by whether it ultimately made money. Separate authority, proper purpose, disclosure and applicable approval requirements. Incorporation also does not automatically confer every operating licence, tax registration or professional permission. A compliance calendar should identify the actual obligation, responsible person and verified timing, without importing another country’s filing dates.',
 'Fictional case: a newly formed education company has incorporation papers but no record of approving a director’s lease with their own property business. It also advertises a regulated service. The weak answer calls everything lawful because the company exists. A strong answer checks the decision powers, conflict and transaction record, then separately investigates the approvals needed for the advertised activity. It distinguishes legal identity from authority and regulatory compliance.',
 [('Incorporation','Creation of an entity through a legally recognised process'),('Director’s authority','Power to act within the governing corporate framework'),('Corporate compliance','Fulfilment of applicable ongoing and operational obligations')],
 'Create a company-document checklist, a conflict-aware board resolution outline and a sourced compliance calendar for the education company.', [
 'Why does incorporation not settle permission to offer a regulated service?|Separate operating and professional requirements may apply|Incorporation automatically grants every licence|Only the founder’s confidence matters|Every registered company is exempt from tax|Entity status and activity-specific compliance are distinct legal questions.',
 'A director contracts with their own business. What must be analysed?|Authority, conflict duties and applicable disclosure or approval|Only whether the company might profit|Only the director’s ownership percentage|Whether the other directors like the proposal|Related-party dealings require attention to powers and duties, not outcome alone.',
 'What supports an accountable corporate decision?|A record of the competent decision, basis and relevant conflict handling|A founder’s job title alone|An undated verbal assurance|An incorporation certificate used as unlimited transaction authority|Decision records help establish who acted and under what lawful power.',
 'How should a corporate compliance calendar be prepared?|From the entity’s actual local obligations and verified timing|By copying every deadline from a foreign company guide|By assuming registration ends all duties|By listing dates without responsible persons or sources|A useful calendar connects each obligation to current governing authority.'
 ], 'szv76fD_ftY',
 'Address entity status, transaction authority, duties and separate regulatory obligations. Link each conclusion to the relevant documents and local legal source.',
 ['Map a fictional company’s founder, shareholders, directors and officers to their distinct roles.', 'Audit a board minute for missing conflict disclosure, authority and decision evidence.'])

add(34, 'Partnership Liability, Agency Authority and Business Dissolution', 'business',
 'Find the applicable Liberian rules on partnerships, authority and winding up. Examine the arrangement and transaction rather than assuming one foreign partnership form applies.',
 'A partnership is a legally relevant business relationship whose classification and consequences depend on governing law. Dissolution concerns ending the relationship; winding up concerns settling its affairs. Agency authority remains important when partners or representatives transact with third parties.',
 'Examine how the enterprise was formed, its participants, contributions, business activity and governing agreement. A shared office or one joint project does not by itself establish every legal feature of a partnership. Distinguish actual authority, representations to outsiders and personal undertakings when identifying who is bound by a transaction.\n\nEnding operations does not automatically erase unpaid obligations. Identify assets, contracts, liabilities, creditor rights and authority to complete remaining business. Record notices, pending work and final accounts under the applicable rules. A partner’s private withdrawal from a group message may differ from a legally effective change communicated as required. Separate internal allocation of loss from an outsider’s enforceable rights, and do not promise that a dissolution announcement cancels debts.',
 'Fictional case: three designers trade together in Monrovia. One orders printing supplies, another leaves and posts “the business is closed”, while customers still await work. The weak answer assumes the post eliminates all liability. The strong answer checks the legal relationship, ordering authority, remaining contracts and debts, and the steps needed for winding up. It distinguishes the partners’ internal disagreement from supplier and customer rights.',
 [('Partnership classification','Legal character of the business relationship'),('Dissolution','Ending the relationship according to the governing framework'),('Winding up','Settling outstanding affairs, assets and obligations')],
 'Prepare a dissolution-and-winding-up plan with an authority matrix, creditor list, outstanding work record and issues requiring verified local advice.', [
 'Why does announcing closure not automatically cancel business debts?|Outstanding obligations and winding-up duties still require legal resolution|All creditors lose rights once a post is published|Internal disagreements repeal contracts|A closed office proves every debt was paid|Dissolution and discharge of third-party obligations are distinct matters.',
 'What distinguishes internal sharing of a loss from liability to a supplier?|The supplier’s rights require their own governing legal and authority analysis|An internal agreement always eliminates outsider rights|Every partner has identical liability in all legal forms|A supplier must accept any later private allocation|Internal arrangements and externally enforceable obligations may differ.',
 'A participant privately withdraws from the venture. What should be checked?|The applicable requirements and effect on existing and later transactions|Whether any message automatically erases all responsibility|Whether withdrawal converts every contract into a gift|Whether remaining participants may ignore all records|The legal effect of withdrawal depends on the relationship, process and relevant transaction timing.',
 'Which product supports orderly winding up?|An asset, liability, authority and unfinished-contract record|Only a logo removal|Only a final social-media post|A promise that creditors will never be contacted|Settling affairs requires a documented account of what remains to be done.'
 ], 'ETVTzDs6mRY',
 'Establish the legal relationship and authority first. Then distinguish dissolution, continuing third-party obligations and the lawful process for settling the enterprise’s affairs.',
 ['Allocate the designers’ unfinished transactions to an authorised winding-up task list.', 'Argue a supplier’s position separately from the participants’ proposed internal loss-sharing arrangement.'])

add(35, 'Sale of Goods, Consumer Contracts and Commercial Risk', 'commercial',
 'Read the Liberian Commercial Code provisions applicable to the transaction. Verify any separate consumer or sector rules rather than importing foreign sales legislation.',
 'A sale-of-goods transaction concerns specified goods and obligations under the applicable contract and law. Title, possession and risk of loss are different concepts. Inspection, conformity and remedies require attention to the actual delivery and contractual framework.',
 'Identify whether the transaction concerns goods, services or a mixture and which rules apply. Establish description, quantity, quality, price, delivery terms and any legally relevant warranties or conditions. A transfer of possession does not always resolve title or allocate every risk. Read the governing provisions and agreement together before concluding who bears a loss in transit.\n\nDocument delivery, inspection, acceptance or rejection and the alleged non-conformity. Distinguish a damaged item from a late but conforming delivery and separate the remedy each allegation supports. A consumer’s practical vulnerability may be relevant to applicable protections, but dissatisfaction alone does not create an unlimited refund rule. Preserve purchase records, notices, condition evidence and efforts to limit further loss. Any proposed risk clause needs its own incorporation and legal-effect analysis.',
 'Fictional case: a cooperative orders solar batteries delivered to Tubmanburg. Some arrive damaged; the seller says risk passed when the carrier collected them, while the cooperative cites a delivery promise. A weak answer assumes physical collection settles every issue. A strong answer identifies the applicable sales framework, agreed delivery and risk terms, carrier records and inspection evidence, then separately analyses conformity and available remedies.',
 [('Title','Legal ownership, distinct from possession'),('Risk of loss','Allocation of responsibility for specified loss under governing rules'),('Conformity','Whether supplied goods meet legally relevant obligations')],
 'Create a delivery-and-risk timeline and a 1,000-word sales remedies memorandum. Include the exact contract wording and missing carrier or inspection records.', [
 'Why does carrier collection not by itself settle responsibility for damaged batteries?|Risk depends on the applicable law and agreed terms, distinct from possession|The carrier becomes owner of every shipment|Every buyer bears all loss before delivery|A delivery promise is never legally relevant|Title, possession and risk must be analysed separately.',
 'What supports a claim that delivered goods were non-conforming?|The agreed or legally required standard and reliable condition evidence|The buyer’s dissatisfaction alone|Only the seller’s advertisement colour|A foreign automatic-refund slogan|A conformity claim connects the actual obligation to the supplied goods.',
 'Why distinguish damaged goods from late delivery?|The breaches, evidence and available remedies may differ|Only damaged goods can involve a contract|Delay automatically proves all quality defects|Both claims always produce an identical award|Different obligations require separate application and remedy analysis.',
 'A transaction includes installation and equipment. What should be checked?|Its classification and the governing rules for the relevant obligations|Whether every mixed contract is legally identical|Whether services eliminate all goods obligations|Whether a title heading alone determines every rule|Mixed transactions require attention to the applicable contractual and statutory framework.'
 ], 'pBZjFomE9cA',
 'Identify the transaction’s classification and obligations, then separate title, possession, risk, conformity and remedies. Use a timeline to connect each disputed event to the governing rule.',
 ['Allocate fictional shipment losses under two different clearly stated contract clauses, reserving local enforceability questions.', 'Prepare an inspection record that separates observations from assumptions about when damage occurred.'])

add(35, 'Insolvency, Creditors and Priority of Claims', 'commercial',
 'Use the Commercial Code and Associations Law materials relevant to the debtor and proceeding. Confirm any current insolvency or sector-specific framework before asserting a priority or enforcement power.',
 'Financial distress may involve inability to meet obligations or another legally defined insolvency condition. A secured claim differs from an unsecured claim. Reorganisation, liquidation and ordinary individual debt enforcement serve different purposes under their governing regimes.',
 'Start by identifying the debtor’s legal form, financial facts and applicable process. A cash shortage, a disputed debt and statutory insolvency are not identical. Examine assets, liabilities, security documents and any current proceeding before recommending an individual collection route. A priority claim must arise from the governing law, not simply from the creditor’s urgency.\n\nFor security, distinguish attachment or underlying validity, required registration or other perfection steps, priority and lawful enforcement. A creditor’s entry in a register is important but does not replace every underlying requirement. A collective proceeding may affect individual remedies, transactions and distribution; verify the actual rules rather than importing a foreign “waterfall”. Consider transactions benefiting insiders, related-party payments and available review while describing unproved wrongdoing as an allegation.',
 'Fictional case: a small manufacturer cannot pay suppliers and wages, while a lender claims equipment security and an owner has recently withdrawn funds. A weak answer pays creditors in the order they complain. A strong answer identifies the applicable process, verifies assets and the security claim, checks legally recognised priorities and examines the owner’s transaction. It does not assume either complete lender priority or equal distribution before the governing conditions are established.',
 [('Secured claim','Claim supported by a legally effective security interest'),('Priority','Legally determined ranking for specified purposes'),('Collective process','Procedure addressing the debtor’s affairs beyond one creditor’s demand')],
 'Prepare a creditor-and-assets schedule, a security-validation checklist and a 900-word options note comparing individual enforcement with an applicable collective route.', [
 'Why does a creditor’s urgency not establish legal priority?|Ranking comes from the applicable law and legally effective rights|The first complaint always receives payment first|Every secured notice guarantees unlimited recovery|A debtor may choose to ignore all creditors|Priority requires an authority-based examination of the claim and process.',
 'What must be checked beyond a collateral registry entry?|The underlying agreement, validity, scope and legally relevant priority conditions|Only the registry logo|A rule that registration cures every defect|Whether the lender wants the asset urgently|Registration and the creation or scope of the secured right are separate questions.',
 'Why distinguish a cash shortage from statutory insolvency?|The applicable legal condition and process require their own criteria|Any delayed invoice proves every insolvency requirement|A profitable product eliminates every debt issue|Only creditors may define insolvency privately|Financial facts must be tested against the governing legal framework.',
 'An owner withdrew funds before a collective process. What is the appropriate analysis?|Investigate authority, timing, beneficiary and any applicable review rule|Assume every withdrawal is lawful because ownership exists|Declare criminal guilt without evidence|Ignore insider transactions categorically|The legal effect depends on the actual transaction and governing rules.'
 ], 's_vTdh_sEeA',
 'Identify the debtor and process, validate each asserted right and source any ranking rule. Avoid replacing the local framework with a memorised foreign distribution order.',
 ['Build a fictional creditor schedule that distinguishes disputed, secured and unsecured claims without prematurely ranking them.', 'Compare an owner withdrawal with an ordinary supplier payment and list the facts required for a lawful review.'])

add(36, 'Public Procurement, Concessions and Accountability', 'procurement',
 'Use the PPCC’s official legal documents, including its 2026 instruments, and verify commencement and applicability to the transaction. Do not rely solely on the earlier 2010 framework.',
 'Public procurement concerns acquisition through a legally regulated public process. A concession may grant specified rights or responsibilities under a distinct arrangement. Accountability requires traceable authority, fair procedures and a record connecting criteria to decisions.',
 'Identify the procuring entity, authorised decision-makers, transaction type and governing instrument. Distinguish planning, solicitation, evaluation, award and contract administration. A procurement’s lawful objective does not remove its process requirements. Set criteria transparently as required, assess responsive evidence against those criteria and record the reasons for decisions.\n\nCompetition and disclosure rules must be read with lawful exceptions rather than reduced to “always choose the lowest price”. An emergency or single-source method requires its own legal basis and supporting facts. Examine conflicts, communications, approval records and the applicable complaint or review process. A concession involving a public resource may raise additional rights, consultation and sector questions. Avoid quoting an outdated numeric threshold without verifying current regulations.',
 'Fictional case: a public office seeks classroom furniture and one evaluator has a financial interest in a bidder. A cheaper offer also fails a stated specification. A weak answer selects it solely on price or privately awards the contract to the interested evaluator’s preferred firm. A strong answer identifies the current rules, documented requirements, conflict handling and lawful evaluation method; explains the proposed decision with evidence; and preserves the review record.',
 [('Procurement','Regulated public acquisition process'),('Concession','Distinct grant or arrangement requiring its own applicable analysis'),('Evaluation record','Documented application of lawful criteria to submitted evidence')],
 'Produce an evaluation matrix, conflict-handling note and 900-word accountability memorandum using the current official PPCC source list.', [
 'Why is “always choose the lowest price” an incomplete procurement rule?|The lawful criteria, responsiveness and governing procedure must also be applied|Price can never be considered|An evaluator’s private interest controls the award|Specifications become irrelevant after bids arrive|Value and lawful responsiveness require more than an isolated price comparison.',
 'An evaluator has an interest in a bidder. What should be addressed?|The conflict and applicable disclosure or participation safeguards|Only whether the bidder is cheap|Only the evaluator’s seniority|A rule that all public officers may privately select relatives|Decision integrity requires handling the relevant conflict through the governing process.',
 'A public office claims an emergency exception. What is required?|The current legal basis and evidence satisfying the exception’s conditions|An emergency label that automatically removes all safeguards|A foreign threshold copied as local law|A promise that no record will be kept|Exceptions must be supported under the applicable framework.',
 'Why check the PPCC’s current instruments before a threshold analysis?|Later amendments and regulations may alter the applicable rules|Older legislation is always automatically repealed in full|A training video fixes all current amounts|Any bidder may choose its preferred threshold|Version and applicability checks prevent outdated rules from determining a real process.'
 ], 'Tel3dnWoQfw',
 'Map each decision to its competent actor, governing requirement and record. Analyse conflicts and exceptions explicitly, and distinguish an award decision from contract performance.',
 ['Evaluate three fictional bids against a stated lawful criteria matrix without changing criteria afterward.', 'Build an audit trail identifying approvals, conflict records, reasons and available complaint steps.'])

add(36, 'Tax Administration, Assessments and Objections', 'tax',
 'Use the Liberia Revenue Authority tax-law and amendment collection. Verify the taxpayer, relevant period, tax type, assessment basis and current objection procedure.',
 'An assessment determines a tax obligation through the applicable legal process. An objection challenges a specified aspect through an authorised route. Tax liability, administrative penalty, interest and enforcement questions should be distinguished rather than added together without authority.',
 'Start with the legal basis, relevant taxpayer and taxable facts. A business’s gross receipts, taxable income and available deductions are different concepts determined by the applicable regime. Reconcile source records to the assessment and identify whether the disagreement concerns facts, classification, calculation or interpretation. Each claim needs evidence and a relevant legal rule.\n\nAn objection should identify the challenged determination, period, grounds, supporting documents and requested correction. Verify current requirements and any effect on payment or enforcement; do not assume every challenge automatically suspends collection. Preserve correspondence and proof of filing, and identify the next review stage through the governing local process. Avoid asserting an exemption, deduction or rate from a foreign tutorial or an obsolete summary.',
 'Fictional case: a shop in Voinjama receives an assessment including sales it says were cancelled and goods it says were purchased for resale. A weak response simply refuses payment. A strong response reconciles the invoices, cancellation and purchase records, identifies the applicable tax rules, explains each disputed adjustment and follows the verified objection route. It keeps the shop’s factual account separate from an established entitlement to a deduction.',
 [('Assessment','Legally made determination of a tax obligation'),('Objection','Authorised challenge to a specified determination'),('Reconciliation','Connection between source records, figures and asserted adjustments')],
 'Prepare a tax-record reconciliation and a fictional objection outline with grounds, sources, evidence, requested correction and a verified procedural checklist.', [
 'What improves an objection to cancelled sales in an assessment?|Records identifying the transactions and the applicable treatment|A blanket refusal with no reasons|A foreign tax rate copied into the response|An assumption that every invoice cancellation creates an exemption|A supported objection links the disputed facts to the governing tax rule.',
 'Why distinguish tax, penalty and interest?|Each component requires its own applicable basis and calculation|They are always identical figures|A tax objection automatically abolishes every penalty|Only interest can ever be disputed|Different components may raise different factual and legal issues.',
 'What must be checked before assuming an objection stops collection?|The effect provided by the applicable local procedure and any relevant order|The taxpayer’s personal expectation alone|A universal rule that all challenges suspend payment|The fact that a letter has been drafted|An objection’s procedural and enforcement consequences require verification.',
 'An expense is asserted to be deductible. What should the learner establish?|The relevant statutory condition and records supporting it|Only that money was spent|Only that the owner prefers a smaller assessment|The deduction shown in another country’s tutorial|Expense evidence and legal deductibility are distinct requirements.'
 ], 'CrmGbCXig4M',
 'State the tax type and period, separate disputed adjustments and apply the exact local provisions to reconciled records. Identify procedural uncertainty before giving a remedial conclusion.',
 ['Reconcile a fictional ledger with an assessment and mark factual versus legal disagreements.', 'Draft a concise objection paragraph that links one claimed adjustment to its supporting records and governing provision.'])

add(37, 'Regional Human-Rights Systems and Complaint Procedures', 'regional-rights',
 'Read the African Commission’s official communication guidance and current rules. Verify the relevant treaty status, competent institution and admissibility conditions for Liberia.',
 'Regional human-rights mechanisms supervise obligations within their particular legal instruments. A communication to a commission differs from an ordinary domestic lawsuit. Jurisdiction, admissibility, merits and remedy are separate stages of analysis.',
 'Identify the right, alleged conduct, responsible state and applicable instrument. Determine which regional body is competent and what legal relationship makes the claim relevant to it. Do not confuse the African Commission with the African Court or assume every institution has identical access rules. Verify party-specific treaty and declaration status where relevant.\n\nA factual complaint should distinguish observed events, allegations, sources and the legal characterisation proposed. Analyse domestic remedies and any recognised exceptions under the applicable guidance; “international” does not mean domestic procedures never matter. Record other proceedings, relevant timing, identification and requested measures accurately. An admissible complaint has not automatically succeeded on the merits, and a recommendation or decision must be described according to the issuing body’s actual powers.',
 'Fictional case: a community alleges a public restriction violated a protected right and wishes to “send it to the African Court” immediately. A weak answer assumes any regional body must hear it. A strong response identifies the governing right and evidence, distinguishes institutions and their access conditions, researches available domestic remedies and the relevant admissibility rules, and prepares a balanced communication outline without guaranteeing a finding.',
 [('Jurisdiction','The body’s competence over the relevant claim'),('Admissibility','Conditions for considering a communication'),('Merits','Whether the alleged violation is established under the governing instrument')],
 'Prepare a fictional regional communication outline with a jurisdiction and admissibility checklist, domestic-remedies chronology, evidence table and precise requested measures.', [
 'Why distinguish the African Commission from the African Court?|Their powers, access conditions and procedures are not interchangeable|Their names create identical jurisdiction|Every complaint automatically becomes a domestic appeal|Only the complainant’s preferred institution matters|Regional institutions must be analysed under their own governing instruments.',
 'What should an account of domestic remedies contain?|The actual procedures used, outcomes and legally relevant exceptions to investigate|A blanket claim that international filing removes every local requirement|A fabricated court result|Only the community’s dissatisfaction|Admissibility analysis requires a verifiable remedies record and applicable conditions.',
 'What does an admissibility conclusion not establish automatically?|Success on the merits of the alleged violation|The need to identify the competent body|The relevance of procedural conditions|The importance of accurate facts|Permission to consider a complaint differs from deciding that a violation occurred.',
 'Why verify party-specific treaty and access status?|The state’s obligations and available route depend on the relevant legal position|All states have identical reservations and declarations|A treaty title alone establishes every institution’s powers|A foreign complaint form proves Liberia’s status|International and regional access must be checked for the actual state and instrument.'
 ], 'sAL5nxTvuIU',
 'Analyse the institution, instrument, jurisdiction, admissibility and merits in sequence. Keep the domestic-remedies record and requested measures distinct from an unsupported promise of relief.',
 ['Compare two regional bodies on a powers-and-access table using official sources.', 'Turn an emotional fictional rights account into a sourced chronology that labels allegations and established facts.'])

add(37, 'Conflict of Laws, Jurisdiction and Foreign Judgments', 'commercial',
 'Research the relevant Liberian statutes, judgments and treaty status for the cross-border transaction. A foreign choice-of-law rule is comparative until its local relevance is established.',
 'Conflict of laws addresses which forum and law apply to a private dispute with cross-border connections and how foreign decisions are recognised or enforced. Governing law, jurisdiction and enforcement are distinct questions.',
 'Map the parties, places of formation and performance, location of property, relevant events and available agreement clauses. A choice-of-law clause does not necessarily confer jurisdiction on a particular court, and a forum clause must be tested for its scope and legal effect. Mandatory rules or other recognised limits may remain relevant under the governing framework.\n\nA foreign judgment’s existence does not establish its immediate enforceability against assets in Liberia. Identify the competent recognition or enforcement route and applicable conditions, including legally relevant finality, jurisdiction, notice or other grounds to examine. Do not retry the merits through a process that does not authorise it. Keep the authentic judgment, service record, contract and asset information separate. International arbitration awards require their own analysis rather than being labelled ordinary foreign court judgments.',
 'Fictional case: a Liberian importer and a foreign supplier choose the supplier’s national law but say nothing about forum. A foreign judgment is later presented against property in Monrovia. A weak answer assumes the law clause settles every court and enforcement question. A strong answer separates governing law, forum competence and recognition, obtains the actual judgment and notice record, and investigates the authorised local route before predicting whether an asset can lawfully be reached.',
 [('Governing law','Law used to determine specified substantive issues'),('Jurisdiction','Competence of the proposed forum'),('Recognition and enforcement','Legal effect and execution of a foreign decision through the applicable route')],
 'Write a 1,000-word cross-border issues memo and draft separate governing-law and forum clauses, explaining limits that drafting alone cannot settle.', [
 'Why does a governing-law clause not necessarily choose a court?|Substantive law and forum competence are distinct questions|Every law clause automatically names a particular courtroom|Courts cannot hear cross-border disputes|A contract eliminates all mandatory procedural rules|Choice of law does not by itself settle jurisdiction.',
 'A creditor holds a foreign judgment against assets in Monrovia. What comes next?|Investigate the competent local recognition or enforcement process|Private seizure based solely on the foreign document|Automatic reopening of every merits issue|An assumption that all foreign decisions are void|Foreign judgments require analysis of their local legal effect and authorised enforcement.',
 'Which record helps examine fair notice in the foreign proceeding?|The authentic service and participation record|The creditor’s preferred summary alone|A copied unrelated contract|The debtor’s nationality alone|Recognition conditions must be tested against evidence of the actual proceeding.',
 'Why distinguish an arbitral award from a foreign court judgment?|Different governing instruments and enforcement routes may apply|Their labels always have identical legal consequences|Every arbitral award is an ordinary criminal verdict|All foreign documents can be executed without a process|The nature of the decision determines the relevant legal analysis.'
 ], 'mnf3MAEqvQA',
 'Use separate headings for forum, governing law and recognition. Identify the transaction’s connections and the local source governing each question.',
 ['Map a fictional transaction’s country connections without assuming one resolves every issue.', 'Compare two contract clauses to identify whether each addresses law, forum, both or neither.'])

add(38, 'Bills of Lading, Charterparties and Maritime Claims', 'business',
 'Read the Liberian Maritime Law and current relevant materials in the Registry library. Check the contract and applicable international instrument rather than assuming foreign shipping rules control.',
 'A bill of lading can serve legally relevant receipt, contract-evidence and document functions depending on its terms and governing law. A charterparty concerns an arrangement for use or employment of a vessel. Cargo interests, carriers, owners and charterers may occupy different legal roles.',
 'Identify the actual contracting parties and documents before allocating responsibility. A ship’s flag does not alone answer who promised carriage or bears a particular cargo loss. Read the bill’s descriptions, signatures, reservations and incorporated terms alongside the charter arrangement where relevant. Distinguish ownership of a vessel from performance of carriage obligations.\n\nFor a claim, establish cargo condition, receipt, handling, delivery and the alleged breach, with a timeline supported by records. Examine notice, limitation, defences and forum only under the applicable framework and verified terms; foreign seminar examples are not local deadlines. A charter dispute about hire or use of the vessel may differ from a cargo claimant’s rights. Keep each party’s obligation and each loss separate to avoid turning every shipping participant into an identical defendant.',
 'Fictional case: equipment arrives at the Freeport damaged. The importer holds a bill of lading signed for a carrier, while a separate owner-charterer dispute concerns delayed hire. A weak answer treats the flag-state registry as the contracting carrier. A strong answer maps the bill and charter parties, checks receipt and delivery condition records, identifies the importer’s actual contractual route and reserves any notice or limitation conclusion pending the governing documents and law.',
 [('Bill of lading','Transport document with functions determined by law and wording'),('Charterparty','Contract concerning use or employment of a vessel'),('Carrier responsibility','Obligations of the legally relevant carriage party')],
 'Prepare a shipping roles map, cargo-condition timeline and 900-word claim outline distinguishing the importer’s issue from the charterer’s dispute.', [
 'Why does a vessel’s flag not alone identify the contractual carrier?|Regulatory registration and carriage obligations are distinct legal connections|The flag automatically signs every bill of lading|All owners, charterers and carriers have identical obligations|A ship’s nationality cancels all contracts|Maritime responsibility requires identification of the actual role and legal undertaking.',
 'What should be obtained before asserting a cargo notice deadline?|The applicable local or international framework and relevant contract terms|Only a foreign seminar’s example|Only the importer’s recollection|A universal deadline assumed for all shipping|Notice and limitation depend on the governing rules and transaction.',
 'Why separate an owner-charterer hire dispute from a cargo claim?|Different parties, obligations and remedies may be involved|Cargo interests always owe the vessel’s hire|A charter disagreement proves cargo damage|The registry is automatically responsible for both|Separate maritime relationships require their own legal analysis.',
 'Which record directly assists a cargo-condition analysis?|Receipt, handling and delivery records identifying the goods and condition|Only a photograph of the vessel’s flag|Only the owner’s corporate logo|Only the port’s name|A supported timeline helps connect damage to the relevant obligation and event.'
 ], 'vVcZP02Y_pw',
 'Map documents and parties before naming a cause of action. Apply the governing carriage or charter rules to the supported timeline and keep separate contractual claims distinct.',
 ['Allocate fictional owner, carrier, charterer and cargo interests to the documents they signed or received.', 'Compare cargo-condition records at receipt and delivery, identifying what they prove and what they leave uncertain.'])

add(38, 'Insurance Claims, Disclosure and Subrogation', 'insurance',
 'Read the Liberia Insurance Act and the relevant policy. Verify current regulation, coverage terms and applicable local authority before asserting a claim or recovery right.',
 'An insurance claim seeks the benefit provided by an applicable policy for an insured event. Indemnity compensates a covered loss within its terms. Subrogation concerns an insurer’s legally recognised recovery position after payment; it is distinct from unlimited ownership of every insured claim.',
 'Analyse the insuring clause, subject matter, period, conditions, exclusions and alleged event in sequence. Premium payment alone does not establish that every loss is covered. Identify what was disclosed at formation and what information or notice the policy and law require for the claim. A disputed omission should be tested under the governing standard rather than treated as automatic forfeiture.\n\nFor recovery after payment, distinguish the insured’s loss, insurer’s payment and any remaining claim against another party. Avoid double recovery for the same compensated loss. Subrogation and contribution address different relationships and require their own conditions. Preserve the full policy, proposal information, incident record, loss calculation and correspondence. Do not assume that a foreign health-insurance lien operates identically under a Liberian property policy.',
 'Fictional case: a delivery business claims for a damaged vehicle after an insured collision and also seeks payment from the other driver. The insurer disputes a policy condition and later offers a partial payment. A weak answer adds both demands together as unrestricted profit. A strong answer investigates coverage and conditions, reconciles the actual loss and payment, and examines any lawful recovery rights while separating compensated and uncompensated amounts.',
 [('Coverage','Risk and benefit within the insuring terms and applicable law'),('Indemnity','Compensation of covered loss within the relevant framework'),('Subrogation','Legally recognised recovery position linked to an insurer’s payment')],
 'Draft a coverage-and-claim checklist and a recovery reconciliation showing loss, policy limits, payment, remaining loss and rights requiring verification.', [
 'Why does paying a premium not prove coverage for every event?|The insuring clause, conditions, exclusions and applicable law define the benefit|All insurance covers every possible loss|Only the claimant’s expectation controls coverage|Payment eliminates all policy wording|Coverage requires the relevant event and claim to fall within the governing terms.',
 'What must be reconciled when both an insurer and another party may pay?|The actual loss, insurance payment and any remaining recoverable amount|A right to receive the full same loss repeatedly|Only which payer replies first|A universal rule that the insured owns every subrogation right|Recovery analysis must distinguish compensated and uncompensated loss.',
 'A disclosure omission is alleged. What should be examined?|The governing disclosure rule, material facts and legal consequence|Automatic forfeiture for any missing word|Automatic coverage despite every relevant omission|A foreign insurer’s preferred practice as Liberian law|The effect of an omission depends on the applicable legal and contractual framework.',
 'Why distinguish subrogation from contribution?|They concern different recovery relationships and requirements|Both simply mean payment of any premium|Every insurer automatically owns every third-party claim|Neither requires a governing legal basis|Different recovery doctrines need their own conditions and party analysis.'
 ], 'htqSRehoTeo',
 'Analyse coverage first, then the claim conditions and quantum, and finally recovery after payment. Keep policy interpretation separate from an assumed entitlement based on expectation.',
 ['Annotate a fictional policy into insuring clause, conditions, exclusions and claim procedure.', 'Prepare a loss-and-payment ledger that prevents counting the same compensated loss twice.'])

add(39, 'Environmental Impact Assessment and Regulatory Enforcement', 'environment',
 'Read Liberia’s Environmental Protection and Management Law and current EPA requirements relevant to the activity. Check the applicable assessment, authorisation, consultation and monitoring process.',
 'Environmental assessment investigates a proposed activity’s impacts and alternatives through the governing process. An authorisation may impose conditions. Regulatory enforcement addresses compliance or breach using legally available powers and procedures.',
 'Identify the activity, location, affected resources and people, and the responsible proponent and regulator. Assessment is more than a report title: examine baseline information, significant effects, alternatives, mitigation, consultation and the decision process required by law. A land right or company registration does not replace environmental requirements.\n\nConditions and monitoring turn an approval into continuing responsibilities. Compare the authorised activity with actual operations and record the evidence of any alleged breach. Enforcement may involve directions, sanctions or proceedings only within the regulator’s lawful powers and procedure. A complaint should distinguish measured harm, allegations and legal questions; a photograph can raise a concern without proving every element. Preserve sampling and inspection provenance, and identify urgent lawful protection where needed without promising a predetermined penalty.',
 'Fictional case: a small processing facility near a river changes its output after an assessment and residents report discoloured water. A weak answer assumes the old approval permits any expansion or that a photograph proves every violation. A strong answer checks the assessed and authorised activity, conditions, actual changes, inspection and sampling evidence, and the appropriate EPA process. It separates regulatory compliance from a possible private damages claim.',
 [('Assessment','Structured examination of impacts and alternatives under the governing process'),('Authorisation condition','Continuing obligation attached to a lawful permission'),('Compliance evidence','Records connecting actual operations to applicable requirements')],
 'Prepare an assessment-and-conditions checklist, monitoring evidence plan and 900-word regulatory memorandum for the facility’s changed activity.', [
 'Why does an existing assessment not automatically authorise every later expansion?|The approved scope and applicable change requirements must be checked|Any report permits all future activity|Land ownership replaces environmental controls|Residents’ views automatically amend the permit|The authorised activity and its conditions determine the compliance question.',
 'What strengthens an environmental complaint beyond a photograph?|Traceable inspection, sampling or other relevant evidence linked to the alleged effect|A larger unverified penalty demand|An assumption that colour alone proves every breach|A copied foreign enforcement order|Reliable provenance and connection to the legal requirement support the claim.',
 'Why separate regulatory enforcement from a private damages claim?|Different powers, parties, elements and remedies may apply|An EPA concern automatically fixes all compensation|Only a company may complain about harm|A permit eliminates every civil issue|Distinct legal routes require their own supporting analysis.',
 'What makes an authorisation condition meaningful in practice?|Monitoring actual operations against the condition and recording compliance|Filing the approval and never reviewing conduct|Assuming company registration proves every condition|Changing the activity without examining the approval|Continuing obligations require evidence of continuing compliance.'
 ], '7DsmEPkHDtM',
 'Identify the assessed and authorised activity, compare actual operations with conditions, and analyse the regulator’s powers and procedure separately from any civil remedy.',
 ['Compare a fictional proposed project, permit conditions and actual changed operations in a three-column matrix.', 'Design a lawful evidence log recording source, location, method and limitations of an environmental observation.'])

add(39, 'Copyright, Trademarks and Patents in Practice', 'liberian-ip',
 'Read Liberia’s Intellectual Property Act in WIPO Lex and check later local instruments, regulations and treaty status relevant to the issue. WIPO’s general guide helps distinguish categories, not establish every local entitlement.',
 'Copyright concerns protected expression; a trademark identifies relevant commercial origin; a patent concerns a qualifying invention under its legal requirements. Ownership, registration, licensing and infringement are separate issues.',
 'Identify the subject matter before choosing a legal category. A business logo may raise trademark and artistic-expression questions; an underlying idea is not the same as the particular expression of it. An invention’s patentability cannot be assumed from novelty in everyday conversation. Examine who created the material, any employment or commissioned-work arrangement and the governing ownership rules.\n\nA licence must permit the intended use within its scope, duration, territory and conditions. Buying a physical object or finding a file online does not necessarily transfer intellectual-property rights. Registration and enforceability depend on the particular right and applicable law; do not claim every category has identical registration requirements. For infringement, compare the asserted right, challenged use, permission and recognised limits or exceptions. A credit line is not a substitute for an effective licence.',
 'Fictional case: a startup uses a downloaded photograph, a similar competitor name and a new device design. A weak answer registers one company name and claims all IP issues are settled. A strong answer separates copyright, trademark and possible patent questions, investigates creators and permissions, searches the relevant records and tests each proposed protection or use against the Liberian framework.',
 [('Copyright','Protection of qualifying expression under applicable law'),('Trademark','Sign identifying relevant commercial origin'),('Patent','Right concerning a qualifying invention under the governing system'),('Licence','Permission within defined scope and conditions')],
 'Prepare an IP audit with separate ownership, permission, registration and infringement columns for the startup’s three assets.', [
 'Why does buying a photograph file not necessarily allow every commercial use?|The rights and licence scope must be established separately from possession|Every download transfers all copyright|A credit line always replaces permission|Company registration makes every image public property|Ownership of a copy and permission to exploit protected material are distinct.',
 'What is the first step before choosing an IP protection strategy?|Identify the subject matter and applicable category of right|Assume all IP is patented|Register any name and ignore every other issue|Treat an idea and its expression as identical|Different forms of intellectual property protect different legally defined subject matter.',
 'Why examine a commissioned-work agreement?|Ownership and permitted use may depend on the applicable law and agreed rights|Payment always transfers every right in every jurisdiction|The creator’s identity is never relevant|An invoice automatically grants a patent|Creation, ownership and licensing require their own legal and factual analysis.',
 'A startup name resembles a competitor’s sign. What should be investigated?|The asserted mark, relevant use and governing infringement or protection rules|Whether any similarity proves every possible violation|Whether copyright and trademark have identical tests|Whether a company certificate overrides all third-party rights|The actual right and challenged use determine the trademark issue.'
 ], 'nXyCyWg6x98',
 'Classify each asset, establish ownership and permissions, then analyse protection and challenged use under the local framework. Keep licence terms and exceptions distinct from credit or possession.',
 ['Sort ten fictional assets into potential IP categories and explain overlap or missing information.', 'Audit a sample licence for intended use, attribution, territory, duration and modification conditions.'])

add(40, 'Conflicts of Interest, Confidentiality and Client Money', 'ethics',
 'Read the Judiciary’s Code of Moral and Professional Ethics and any applicable current rules. The exercises simulate professional issues; the course does not confer practising authority.',
 'A conflict involves interests or duties that may impair proper professional responsibility. Confidentiality concerns protection of information. Client money is property handled for a client or another person, whose permitted use and records must be governed by the applicable duties.',
 'Identify who is or was the client, the matter, relevant duties and any personal or competing interest. A shared objective today does not guarantee that two parties’ interests will remain aligned. Determine whether an applicable rule permits informed consent or requires refusal or withdrawal; do not assume consent cures every conflict.\n\nConfidentiality requires attention to recipients, channels, devices and indirect identification, not only removal of a name. Protect information learned in a professional relationship according to the governing duties and permitted exceptions. Funds held for another person cannot be treated as unrestricted working capital. Separate authority to receive money, permission to use it, accounting and repayment or disbursement. An emergency personal need does not create a right to borrow from a protected fund.',
 'Fictional case: a lawyer is asked to advise both seller and buyer, while the buyer reveals a private concern about the seller’s documents. A deposit is received and the lawyer proposes using it temporarily for office rent. A weak answer accepts both arrangements because everyone trusts the lawyer. A strong answer separately checks conflicting duties, handling of information, any legally permissible consent and the authority and accounting for the deposit.',
 [('Conflict','Competing interest or duty affecting proper responsibility'),('Informed consent','Consent meeting the applicable conditions where legally permitted'),('Client money','Property handled for another within governing authority and duties')],
 'Write an ethics decision note covering acceptance or referral, confidentiality safeguards and deposit handling. Include the relevant local rules and facts requiring clarification.', [
 'Why does trust between the parties not eliminate a professional conflict?|Duties and competing interests must still satisfy the applicable rules|Personal trust overrides every professional duty|Only written disputes can create conflicts|Consent is unnecessary whenever the parties are friendly|A professional relationship needs an independent duties analysis.',
 'When is consent a sufficient response to a conflict?|Only where the governing rule permits it and its conditions are met|Whenever one party says the arrangement is convenient|For every conflict without disclosure|Whenever fees would otherwise be lost|Some conflicts or circumstances may not be cured by consent.',
 'Why is temporarily using a deposit for office rent problematic?|The authority and duties for money held for another must be respected|Any professional may borrow all client funds privately|Temporary use never requires records|A future repayment promise automatically creates permission|Client property is not unrestricted personal or business working capital.',
 'What confidentiality risk can remain after removing names?|Context or other details may still identify the person or matter|Anonymous wording always removes every risk|Only typed names can reveal identity|Disclosure through a public device is automatically authorised|Confidentiality concerns identifiable information and lawful handling, not names alone.'
 ], 'IQnqbF2AT4c',
 'Separate the conflict, information and funds issues. Identify the applicable duty, available lawful response and a practical record showing how the duty will be met.',
 ['Run a fictional conflict check involving a current client, former client and personal business interest.', 'Design a deposit ledger and communications plan that preserve authority, traceability and confidentiality.'])

add(40, 'Appellate Advocacy, Written Submissions and Oral Argument', 'rules',
 'Read current Liberian Supreme Court and other applicable appellate rules. Work from a public judgment or fictional record and verify local filing and presentation requirements.',
 'Appellate advocacy presents a legally available challenge or defence of a decision through issues, standards, record and remedy. Written submissions develop the argument; oral argument addresses its decisive points and the court’s questions.',
 'Identify the review issue before retelling the entire dispute. Explain the governing rule and standard, the challenged reasoning or procedural act, the supporting record and why the alleged error matters. Distinguish correcting an error of law from asking a reviewing court to exercise powers it does not possess. Accurate record references and fair treatment of adverse authority are essential.\n\nOral argument is a conversation directed at the court’s decision, not a memorised speech immune to questions. Answer the question asked, state candid limits and return to the logical route to the requested relief. Concede an immaterial weakness rather than inventing evidence. A respondent should defend the actual decision on available grounds while respecting the record and procedure. Practise a concise opening, a response to a difficult question and a final statement of the lawful order sought.',
 'Fictional case: a supplier challenges a judgment interpreting a delivery clause, but the record contains a later signed variation favourable to the respondent. A weak appeal ignores that document and asks the court to invent a new bargain. A strong submission identifies the review question, addresses the variation candidly, explains the governing interpretation argument and requests relief within the court’s powers. In oral practice the learner answers a bench question about the adverse document directly.',
 [('Review issue','Question the appellate process can lawfully determine'),('Record reference','Pinpoint identification of relevant material in the record'),('Requested relief','Specific lawful order sought from the reviewing court')],
 'Prepare a 1,200-word mock appellate submission, a five-minute oral argument and written answers to five challenging bench questions.', [
 'What makes an appellate issue more useful than a general complaint?|It identifies a reviewable error, governing rule and material record|It repeats dissatisfaction without a legal ground|It asks the court to ignore adverse documents|It assumes every appeal is an unrestricted new trial|An appellate argument must fit the authorised review process.',
 'How should an advocate address the signed variation adverse to their argument?|Explain its actual relevance and develop the argument candidly|Hide it from the record summary|Invent a contradictory witness|Assume oral confidence removes the document|Candour and accurate reasoning require engagement with adverse material.',
 'What is a sound response to a difficult bench question?|Answer it directly within the record and acknowledge any limit|Continue a memorised speech without responding|Claim a source says something it does not|Promise facts outside the record|Oral advocacy should assist the court’s reasoning through accurate responses.',
 'Why specify the lawful order sought on appeal?|The remedy must fit the reviewing court’s powers and the established error|Any dissatisfied party may demand any imaginable order|A long speech substitutes for requested relief|An error automatically decides every remaining issue|Effective advocacy connects the review issue to an available remedial outcome.'
 ], 'JzKExC2MJsQ',
 'Build a chain from reviewable issue to rule, record, materiality and relief. Rehearse direct answers to counterarguments rather than relying on recitation.',
 ['Convert a fictional trial dispute into three candidate appellate issues and eliminate those unsupported by the record.', 'Run an oral-argument exercise where a partner interrupts with an adverse-authority or remedy question.'])

add(41, 'Legal Positivism, Natural Law and African Legal Pluralism', 'constitution',
 'Use the Constitution and verified local legal materials to connect jurisprudential arguments with actual institutions. Theories help explain legal reasoning but do not themselves enact a local rule.',
 'Legal positivist theories examine law’s social sources and institutional recognition. Natural-law theories connect law and moral reasoning in different ways. Legal pluralism studies interacting normative orders; it does not mean every claimed community norm overrides superior law.',
 'State a theory carefully before evaluating it. Positivism does not require approving every valid rule morally; natural-law approaches are not one identical doctrine; pluralism is not an excuse to ignore constitutional safeguards. Distinguish the descriptive question “what is legally recognised?” from the evaluative question “is the rule just?” and the institutional question “who may change or apply it?”.\n\nUse a concrete conflict to test each theory’s explanatory value. Identify statutory and customary claims, constitutional limits and affected interests, then explain how competing accounts of authority interpret the situation. Avoid treating “African law” as a single uniform set of customs. Communities, histories and recognised institutions differ. A strong essay fairly presents an opposing theory and states what evidence or authority would limit its argument rather than declaring one label the answer to every dispute.',
 'Fictional case: a community practice excludes a group from participation in a local decision, while an affected person invokes a constitutional protection. A weak essay says either “custom is always superior” or “every custom is meaningless”. A strong essay separates recognition, moral evaluation and superior-law limits, explains how different theories frame the dispute, and returns to verified Liberian authority for any legal conclusion.',
 [('Legal validity','Status under the relevant system of legal recognition'),('Moral evaluation','Reasoned assessment of justice or legitimacy'),('Legal pluralism','Study of interacting normative orders and their institutional relations')],
 'Write a 1,200-word comparative jurisprudence essay using the fictional practice. Present two theories fairly and distinguish their explanatory claims from the local legal conclusion.', [
 'Why does legal positivism not necessarily endorse every recognised rule morally?|Describing legal validity differs from approving a rule’s justice|Positivism requires praise of every law|Every valid rule automatically resolves every moral question|Moral criticism creates legislation immediately|The theory’s source analysis and moral evaluation are distinct matters.',
 'What is a responsible pluralist analysis of a customary claim?|Identify the particular norm, institution, recognition and superior-law limits|Assume every community has identical rules|Treat all customs as automatically supreme|Treat pluralism as permission to ignore rights|Pluralism examines specific interacting norms rather than a universal override.',
 'Why distinguish theory from the legal source deciding a Liberian claim?|A theoretical explanation does not itself enact or repeal the governing rule|An essay title is binding judicial authority|Every philosopher’s view is a local statute|No legal conclusion can ever use theory|Jurisprudence informs reasoning while operative local authority remains necessary.',
 'What strengthens a comparative jurisprudence essay?|A fair opposing account applied to concrete facts and recognised limits|A list of theory labels with no application|An assumption that all African communities are uniform|A claim that one slogan resolves every dispute|Theories should be explained and tested rather than merely named.'
 ], 'd6JYlhz0Q-k',
 'Define each theory, apply it to the same conflict and evaluate its strengths and limits. Keep the operative constitutional conclusion distinct from the essay’s moral or explanatory preference.',
 ['Classify fictional statements as legal-status claims, moral evaluations or institutional questions.', 'Debate a community-rule dispute from two theories while jointly identifying the legal authority neither theory can replace.'])

add(41, 'Arbitration Agreements, Awards and Settlement Enforcement', 'civil',
 'Read the Civil Procedure Law provisions on arbitration and applicable court rules. For an international issue, verify treaty status and the relevant local enforcement framework.',
 'An arbitration agreement submits covered disputes to a legally recognised arbitral process. An award is the tribunal’s decision. Recognition, enforcement or challenge through a court is different from freely retrying the entire dispute on appeal.',
 'Examine consent, capacity, parties, subject matter, scope and governing procedural terms. A dispute-resolution heading does not by itself prove a valid agreement covering every later dispute. Identify how the tribunal is constituted, notice is given and the parties can present their cases under the applicable framework. A settlement remains an agreement whose legal effect and enforcement require their own analysis.\n\nAn award must be distinguished from mediation advice, an expert report or a private invoice. Read its operative relief and the legal basis for recognition, enforcement or challenge. Review grounds and procedure come from the governing instruments; dissatisfaction with the result does not automatically create a full merits rehearing. For a foreign award, verify the applicable route and party-specific treaty position before invoking any convention. Keep the agreement, notices, tribunal record, award and payment information together.',
 'Fictional case: a supplier contract contains an arbitration clause, but one party says the disputed later service order was outside it. After an award, that party refuses to pay and asks for an unrestricted new trial. A weak answer assumes the clause covers everything and all court review is impossible. A strong answer analyses scope, consent, the actual procedural record, operative relief and the local grounds and route for enforcement or challenge.',
 [('Arbitration agreement','Legally effective submission of covered disputes to arbitration'),('Award','Decision issued through the arbitral process'),('Enforcement or challenge','Court process under the governing conditions and grounds')],
 'Draft a dispute-resolution clause comparison and an award-file checklist. Write a 1,000-word memorandum distinguishing scope, process, enforcement and possible challenge.', [
 'Why does an arbitration heading not establish coverage of every dispute?|The clause’s consent, scope and legal validity must be analysed|Any heading automatically overrides every statutory limit|Every later transaction has identical parties and terms|Mediation and arbitration are always the same process|Coverage depends on the actual agreement and applicable framework.',
 'What distinguishes an arbitral award from mediation advice?|An award is a decision through an arbitral process rather than a proposed voluntary resolution|Any mediator’s suggestion automatically becomes an award|Every private invoice is an arbitral decision|An award requires no governing agreement or procedure|The nature and legal basis of the instrument determine its effect.',
 'A party dislikes an award’s result. What must be checked before court challenge?|The legally recognised grounds and proper local procedure|A universal entitlement to an unrestricted new merits trial|A rule that no award can ever be questioned|The losing party’s preferred timetable alone|Review or challenge is defined by the governing framework.',
 'What is required before relying on a convention for a foreign award?|Its relevant party status and local enforcement operation|Only the convention’s familiar title|A foreign party’s unrelated reservation|An assumption that every state has identical obligations|Treaty application and domestic procedure require specific verification.'
 ], '7oXSxc3TomA',
 'Answer agreement scope, arbitral process and court effect separately. Use the actual award and local review grounds instead of treating arbitration as either unlimited immunity or an automatic new trial.',
 ['Compare mediation, arbitration and court litigation clauses for consent, decision-making and enforcement.', 'Audit a fictional award file for missing agreement, notice, operative relief and payment records.'])

EXTRA_PROJECTS = [
 ('Civil and Criminal Litigation File', 'Use two fictional files: a civil equipment claim and a contested criminal allegation. Keep their burdens, procedures and remedies separate.',
  ['A civil pleading and provisional-relief outline with supporting evidence', 'A criminal charge-and-disclosure checklist and individual elements chart', 'A 1,200-word comparative procedure memorandum with verified local authorities']),
 ('Commercial, Employment and Regulatory Practice Portfolio', 'A fictional training enterprise plans to incorporate, employ staff, buy goods and bid for a public contract.',
  ['A corporate authority and conflict checklist plus a sourced compliance calendar', 'Employment terms, pay-reconciliation and safety checklists', 'A sales risk matrix and current PPCC evaluation-and-accountability note']),
 ('Estates, Community Land and Rights Clinic Simulation', 'A fictional community seeks guidance concerning disputed inherited property, representation and a proposed development.',
  ['A simulated interview chronology and confidentiality plan', 'An estates-and-land authority matrix and community-participation issues register', 'A 1,200-word balanced legal memorandum and a plain-language public explainer']),
 ('Professional Moot Court and Final Legal Opinion', 'Use a fictional appellate record involving a company supply contract, a disputed variation and a conflict of interest. Argue both sides before writing a reasoned opinion.',
  ['Two 1,200-word appellate submissions with primary authorities and record pinpoints', 'Two seven-minute oral arguments plus answers to five bench questions per side', 'A 1,200-word reasoned opinion, eight-project portfolio index and reflective self-assessment'])
]
